Document preparation was performed using the excellent freeware word-processing program \b Jarte\b0  developed by Carolina Road Software, LLC.  Please be advised that attempts to use various Microsoft products will destroy key line formatting and is not recommended.\par
\par
This document is a guide to the P?S/8 keyboard monitor and integrated line editor.  All available commands are documented sufficiently for beginning users to get started using P?S/8 for common text editing, initiating program execution and related tasks.\par
\par
Conventions used in this document.\par
\par
All commands are given in upper-case characters; as an accommodation, lower-case characters are "folded" to the upper-case equivalent as typed.  As such, the commands are shown in all uppercase.  With the exception of the R command, all commands require the first two characters as shown; additional characters are allowed and will be ignored.  Commands will be shown with certain optional trailing characters enclosed in braces indicating the usual meaning of the command; these are provided for clarification purposes only.\par
\par
Commands that access files by name.\par
\par
Certain commands allow optional arguments that are file names taken from the standard P?S/8 directory structure known as the Tiny File System [TFS].  Where this usage applies, the command will be followed by \cf1\i filename\cf0\i0 .  [Note: The actual TFS file names are up to six upper-case characters.]\par
\par
In general, file-oriented commands can also reference the special positional multiple-purpose files known as % and $ [which are located near the physical start of every system device unit for faster access].\par
\par
Logical device unit access.\par
\par
Many commands indicate an optional \cf1\i :u \cf0\i0 suffix.  The current logical unit in effect can be updated using an appended :0 through :7 corresponding to the desired logical device unit.\par
\par
In general, commands that allow the optional \cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  suffix and optional file name arguments will allow the \cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  suffix on the passed file names.  [Note: Only commands for program execution allow multiple file names to be specified; the optional \cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  suffix is allowed on the program name and every specified input or output file name as required.]  All commands and file name options will indicate the optional \cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  suffix where applicable.\page Logical unit interpretation.\par
\par
Logical units are processed by the system device handler in a hardware-dependent manner.  Representative system configurations are described below:\par
\par
1)\tab TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 DECtapes.\par
\par
\tab The logical units refer to the physical DECtape drive tape units \tab which can be changed using drive-mounted unit-select switches.  \tab The system device handler uses the current logical unit directly.\par
\par
2)\tab TC12/TU55/TU56 LINCtapes.\par
\par
\tab The logical units refer to the physical LINCtape drive tape units \tab which can be changed by using drive-mounted unit-select switches.  \tab The system device handler uses the logical unit in effect \tab directly; this is analogous to the TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 system.\par
\par
3)\tab TD8E/TU56 DECtapes.\par
\par
\tab The low-order bit of the logical unit is the only information \tab used by the system device handler to select the physical drive.  \tab Thus, all references are to either drive 0 [for all even logical \tab units] and drive 1 [for all odd logical units].  The physical \tab DECtape drive tape units can be selected as in the \tab TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 system; however, only drives 0 and 1 can be \tab accessed.\par
\par
\tab There are two variations of TD8E system support:\par
\par
\tab a)\tab The system requires the MR8E-C ROM that occupies locations \tab\tab\tab 77400-77777.  This system will run with 4K of read/write \tab\tab\tab memory in field 0; the largest configuration is 28K [due to \tab\tab the presence of the ROM in field 7].\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: System device handler support for additonal drive \tab\tab\tab units is not possible for the MR8E-C ROM-based system due to \tab\tab internal coding restrictions within the ROM itself.\par
\par
\tab b)\tab The system requires 8K of memory in field 0 and field \i x\i0  \tab\tab\tab where \i x\i0  is the highest available field [1 through 7].  Any \tab\tab\tab unused extended memory [if more than 8K is present] is \tab\tab\tab available without restriction.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: Future versions of the TD8E system device handler may \tab\tab support up to four TD8E controllers.  Each TD8E adds two \tab\tab\tab additional drive units [0-1 for the primary controller, 2-3 \tab\tab for the second controller, 4-5 for the third controller and \tab\tab 6-7 for the fourth controller].  In such a configuration, \tab\tab\tab the system device handler will use the logical unit in \tab\tab\tab effect directly.  This configuration would be analogous to \tab\tab\tab the TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 system with the restriction that \tab\tab\tab each TU56 can only select one designated drive pair.\page 4)\tab RX01/RX8E diskettes.\par
\par
\tab The basic RX01 configuration is a single pair of 8" diskette \tab drives referred to as logical unit 0 and 1.  For this form of \tab system configuration, the low-order bit of the logical unit is \tab the only information used to select the physical drive.  As in \tab the case of the TD8E/MR8E-C configuration all even logical units \tab refer to drive 0 and all odd logical units refer to drive 1.\par
\par
\tab Supported drives include the RX01/RX02/RX03, the two-drive \tab variant of the DSD-210, the DSD-440 and the two-drive variant of \tab any VT78, DECmate I and DECmate II RX278 option board systems.\par
\par
\tab Some of these configurations support up to four drives such as \tab the VT78, DECmate I, DECmate II RX278 with two RX01/RX02/RX03 \tab drive pairs and the four-drive variant of the DSD-210.  On these \tab systems, the two low-order bits of the logical unit are used to \tab select the desired drive unit.  The high-order bit of the logical \tab unit is ignored; for example, all references to logical unit 7 \tab are identical to references to drive unit 3.\par
\par
5)\tab RK8E/RK05 and other mapped disk drives.\par
\par
\tab On these systems, a portion of a disk [or perhaps the entire disk \tab drive] maps to a logical unit.  Depending on exact configuration, \tab the RK8E/RK05-based system may contain all eight drives on a \tab single disk drive.  Certain configurations support logical units \tab 0-3 on one physical drive and logical units 4-7 on another drive.\par
\tab Systems configured for four drive support will address two \tab logical drive units on each of up to four RK05 drives.  The \tab largest configuration can address each physical RK05 drive as its \tab own logical unit.  [Note: Future support could include the RKS8E \tab controller to support up to eight drives.]\par
\par
\tab On all variations of system configuration, mapped systems support \tab each logical drive unit uniquely [as is the case in the \tab TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 system, except certain mapped logical units \tab may\tab reside on the same physical disk and cannot be separately \tab dismounted].\par
\par
\tab Several additional devices are supported by P?S/8.  Logical drive \tab unit and other information can be obtained from documents \tab specific to the system device handler that applies.\page Commands that reference line numbers.\par
\par
Certain commands operate on text specified by line number within the edit buffer of the keyboard monitor and integrated editor.  Until the contents of the edit buffer are saved to a named file on the system storage device, the edited text is merely a tentative file image within the edit buffer.  If a file loaded into the edit buffer is modified, all changes are tentative until the edit buffer is written to a file on the storage device.  While line numbers are permanently assigned, various commands are available to change the line number values within the edit buffer; writing to a permanent file updates all file contents including the line numbers.\par
\par
Command parameters expressed as a range of line numbers.\par
\par
Certain commands use an optional range of line numbers [indicated in the command description as \i line-number-range\i0 ] that determine the scope of the command operation:\par
\par
1) nnnn\tab\tab The stated line nnnn is selected; if this line does not \tab\tab\tab exist, no operation occurs.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab One such command is the LI[st] command, which outputs \tab\tab\tab\tab the line number and contents of specified lines to the \tab\tab\tab system console terminal.  For example, \cf2\highlight1\f2 LIST 1000\cf0\highlight0\f0  \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab outputs line 1000 [if line 1000 exists].\par
\par
2) ssss-\tab\tab The operating range for the command is line ssss \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab through the end of the file.  Line ssss need not exist \tab\tab\tab and serves only as a limiting value.  If no lines exist \tab\tab\tab within this range, no operation occurs.  For example, \tab\tab\tab\tab\cf2\highlight1\f2 LIST 500-\cf0\highlight0\f0\tab outputs lines 500 and higher through the end \tab\tab\tab of the file.\par
\par
3) -ffff\tab\tab The operating range for the command is the beginning of \tab\tab\tab the file through line ffff.  Line ffff need not exist \tab\tab\tab\tab and serves only as a limiting value.  If no lines exist \tab\tab\tab within this range, no operation occurs.  For example, \tab\tab\tab\tab\cf2\highlight1\f2 LIST -2000\cf0\highlight0\f0  outputs all lines in the file through line \tab\tab\tab\tab 2000.\par
\par
4) ssss-ffff\tab The operating range for the command is lines ssss \tab\tab\tab\tab through ffff.  Lines ssss and ffff need not exist and \tab\tab\tab\tab serve merely as limiting values.  If no lines exist \tab\tab\tab\tab within this range, no operation occurs.  For example, \tab\tab\tab\tab\cf2\highlight1\f2 LIST 300-700\cf0\highlight0\f0  outputs all lines that fall in the range \tab\tab\tab\tab of 300 through 700.\par
\par
5) <none>\tab\tab If \i line-number-range\i0  is omitted, the operating range \tab\tab\tab\tab for the command is all lines within the file.  If the \tab\tab\tab\tab file is empty, no operation occurs.  For example, \cf2\highlight1\f2 LIST\cf0\highlight0\f0  \tab\tab\tab outputs all lines in the file.\page Command parameters expressed as a starting line number and line number increment.\par
\par
Certain commands take an optional line number parameter [and perhaps an optional line number increment].  This is indicated in the command description as \i optional-starting-line-number\i0  and \i optional-line-increment\i0 .  If neither parameter is present, the default starting line number is 100.  The default line increment is 10 and can only be changed if the starting line number parameter is present.\par
\par
For example, the RE\{sequence\} command reorders the line numbers in a file without changing the textual contents of the lines.  The command \cf2\highlight1\f2 RE\cf0\highlight0\f0  [without parameters] will reorder all line numbers starting at 100; subsequent lines are incremented by 10.  \cf2\highlight1\f2 RE 1000 50\cf0\highlight0\f0  will reorder all line numbers starting at 1000; all subsequent lines are incremented by 50.  The command \cf2\highlight1\f2 RE 2000\cf0\highlight0\f0  will reorder all line numbers starting at 2000; all subsequent lines are incremented by 10.\par
\par
Command parameters expressed by additional means.\par
\par
Certain commands use other methods for stating parameters that are unique to each command; as required, documentation will be provided within each command description including specific examples.\par
\par
In the interest of overall brevity, certain command descriptions may reference other commands with expanded description elsewhere; this should always be clearly understood due to the format of the command that applies; it may be necessary to first reference the description of other command[s] to better understand the current command description.  [This is unavoidable as many of the commands are inter-related.]\par
\par
For example, the RE\{sequence\} command documentation refers to the documentation of the LA\{st\} command due to the way file resequencing is implemented [which includes an automatic equivalent of the LA\{st\} command].\page\f2\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f0\par
History of the P?S/8 command processor and fundamental design issues.\par
\par
P?S/8 is a direct outgrowth of the predecessor operating system known as the R-L Monitor System [named for the principal authors' initials] or MS/8 [the name used when it was submitted to DECUS].  This more primitive system uses the basic concept of commands with only the first two characters significant, allowing additional optional characters that are ignored  [Note: While the R-L Monitor System allows six character file names, system program names are also limited to two characters; additional program name characters are ignored.]\par
\par
The R-L Monitor System was cosmetically modified [the names of certain commands were changed using trivial binary patches while retaining the command functionality] as a stopgap measure incorporated into the early release of DIBOL-8.  This temporary official DEC product, which is similar at the command level to future releases of systems supporting the DIBOL-8 compiler, had only the modest goals of supporting the earliest DIBOL-8 compiler and the ability to load the binary output of compilation.\par
\par
Note: All of the systems described here use the same binary output format internally, thus identical loading techniques apply.  The BIN system utility program exists throughout DIBOL-8, the R-L Monitor System and all releases of P?S/8 albeit with slightly different command syntax due to the evolving nature of the command execution process within each system.  P?S/8 adds many additional features and operating modes to the BIN utility; when only file names are passed to BIN, the functionality is identical to The R-L Monitor System and DIBOL-8.  Binary files from these systems can be copied to P?S/8 using available commands and utilities.  The R-L Monitor System implementation of BIN is limited to 4K memory support only; the binary file format supports 32K memory as implemented only in P?S/8.\par
\par
The largest weakness of DIBOL-8 [which would be overcome in later releases which became known as COS-300/310 or Commercial Operating System Version 3] was the primitive nature of initiating program execution of the compiler and related utility programs; the only information that can be passed to any system program are the contents of user-specified input files [binary output is directed to an implied portion of the TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 DECtape, the only device supported in the original DIBOL-8] and a 12-bit octal value expressed on the command-line as =\i xxxx\i0  where \i xxxx\i0  is any 4-digit octal number.  [Note: For backward compatibility, P?S/8 internally supports the =\i xxxx\i0  parameter identically to the predecessor systems.]\par
\par
P?S/8 and COS-300/310 were developed independently but on a parallel track to improve execution processing by implementing a compact form of what is known in other DEC systems as \b Concise Command Language\b0  [\b CCL\b0 ].  In P?S/8, this includes the ability to pass input and output files and option switches.  The =\i xxxx\i0  value, originally only used to specify a starting address to the binary loader, is now used by several programs for various purposes.\page P?S/8 initially retained consistency with the R-L Monitor System command set where possible.  As a result, a conflict arose between some system program names and the command names.  [Note: As described above, the R-L Monitor System only allows two characters for system program names as well as command names.  Both P?S/8 and COS-300/310 allow up to six character system program names where additional\par
characters [for program names that are exactly six characters] are ignored which is analogous to the way command names are ignored beyond the first two characters in all of these systems.]\par
\par
The DIBOL-8 system command names were chosen to allow the execution of the compiler and other projected system utilities avoiding anomaly between these program names and command names [regarding the first two characters].  Execution can always be initiated by execution commands [R or RU\{n\}] to resolve any conflict that could arise; however, the goal was to avoid the need to routinely resort to this for all common system programs [as well as eliminate certain forms of overhead which is quite noticeable on tape-based systems].\par
\par
Eventually, a parallel design change was gradually made to P?S/8 over the course of several releases resulting in total consistency between P?S/8 and COS-300/310.  For example, the original R-L Monitor System PA\{use\} command is especially useful during BATCH operations:\par
\par
.PAUSE TELL OPERATOR TO SETUP PRINTER THEN PRESS ANY KEY TO CONTINUE\par
\par
The BATCH process is paused; the entire command line is shown on the system console [even if echoing of commands is suppressed]. Execution continues after any key is pressed on the system console keyboard.\par
\par
As originally implemented in DIBOL-8 [and retained in the later releases of COS-300/310], the P?S/8 command name for this function was changed to PL\{ease\}:\par
\par
.PLEASE SETUP THE PRINTER THEN PRESS ANY KEY TO CONTINUE\par
\par
The general design reasoning for this change was to allow concise command execution of an implementation of the PAL assembler [which is generally found in all of these systems, although the specific assembler program referenced may be a completely different implementation of the PDP-8 Assembly Language utility].  By avoidance of a command name expressed as PA\{use\}, the assembler program can be initiated by the PAL program name, thus avoiding the need to use R PAL [or perhaps RU\{n\} PAL] and the attendant overhead.\par
\par
Known surviving implementations of P?S/8 were created circa 1976 where the command names were in transition [without direct contact with the DIBOL-8 or COS-300/310 development process].  The command names used are somewhat different from the corresponding commands of the R-L Monitor System; however, this release is not consistent with the command names of later releases such as Version 8Z [created circa 1989].  This newer release is being made available as part of an ongoing development process adding additional internal features in preparation for new features such as the P?S/8 SHELL overlay.\page Abort character and other control character considerations.\par
\par
In general, all of the operating systems described here support the \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 C\highlight0  character as a general abort and return to the keyboard monitor [by reboot if the abort is detected within a conforming program that will exit to location 07600].  Within the keyboard monitor, pressing \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 C\highlight0  will generally abort an incomplete command being typed [perhaps in auto-sequenced line number input mode].  The original character to accomplish this was \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 D\highlight0  as used in the R-L Monitor System and early P?S/8 releases.  In newer releases of P?S/8, the keyboard monitor abort character was changed to \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 Z\highlight0  to be more consistent with other DEC operating systems.\par
\par
As first implemented in the R-L Monitor System, when the keyboard monitor is accepting input [and subject to abort or other terminal control characters], it is possible to press \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 R\highlight0  or \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 T\highlight0 .  The disposition of these characters is device-dependent.  On the TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 DECtape system, \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 R\highlight0  will rewind the current logical unit allowing tape motion to overlap with further command\par
input; the tape will stop in the reverse end zone automatically.  Pressing \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 T\highlight0  will rewind the tape, then the tape will be forced off the takeup reel.  If the selected drive is type TU55, the brakes will be locked; the keyboard monitor resumes control after the tape is fully unloaded.\par
\par
Current command set issues.\par
\par
As of P?S/8 Version 8Y, all commands are completely consistent with the corresponding COS-300/310 command set.  Many of the utilities planned over the course of time are available in this release with the intended program names.  For example:\par
\par
Both COS-300/310 and P?S/8 support an integrated line editor within the command processor.  In both systems, the LN\{umber\} command is used to automatically number text input; the former command for this purpose was the SE\{quence\} command.  By making this change, both systems support a SET utility to adjust system parameters [without resorting to using the R or RU\{n\} command].  [Note: In the 1976 P?S/8 Version 8P release, the forerunner utility is somewhat less advanced and known as the VARY program to avoid command anomaly.]\par
\par
Appendix B covers all changes known to have been considered [whether\par
implemented or not] for the P?S/8 keyboard monitor and integrated line editor command set.  It is conceivable some of the specific issues raised might be revisited in future P?S/8 releases.\page Command documentation release-related issues.\par
\par
Due to continued interest in older releases of P?S/8, description of the 1976 P?S/8 command set is included; description for the commands of P?S/8 Version 8Z follows.  [Note: Little or no further command name changes are contemplated; all additional work will be directed towards changes to either new or existing system utility programs or in preparation for the optional P?S/8 SHELL overlay currently under development; the P?S/8 SHELL uses a completely different command processor to implement alternate commands consistent with other DEC operating systems found primarily on other computer models.]\par
\par
P?S/8 release information can be obtained as follows:\par
\par
1) \tab The 1976 release is P?S/8 Version 8P; there are no specific \tab utilities to determine this; P?S/8 developers can derive earlier \tab release versions by inspection of various internal storage \tab locations in a designated system block.\par
\par
\tab In Version 8P [and all earlier Version 8\i x \i0 releases], the version \tab is stored in relative location 12 of block 0001 as two six-bit \tab characters.  This convention was changed in Version 8U to \tab accommodate newer internal bootup procedures required by certain \tab newly-added system devices.  [Note: The only known surviving \tab Version 8P system is for the TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 DECtape.]\par
\par
2)\tab As of Version 8U, the version is now stored in relative location \tab 20 of block 0001 in the same format.  The reason for the change \tab is that relative location 12 is loaded into location 00012 and is \tab now used as a preloaded auto-index register during certain \tab internal bootup routines; location 00020 is the first available \tab location beyond the auto-index registers.  Other than permanently \tab storing the system version, this location has no permanent \tab contents requirement.\par
\par
\tab Note: Internal knowledge of P?S/8 is no longer required to obtain \tab version information.  Newer versions include the SYSTAT\{us\} \tab utility which provides overall system version and certain \tab additional system-wide configuration settings.\par
\par
\tab As of this writing, the currently available release is Version 8Z \tab including several binary patches applied by Charles Lasner, lead \tab program designer of P?S/8.  Known bugs have been corrected \tab pending source code cleanup and a new system build to Version 9A.\par
\par
A list of all available system programs can be obtained as follows:\par
\par
1)\tab For any release through Version 8X:\par
\par
\tab .R SYSTYP\par
\par
2)\tab For Version 8Y and newer:\par
\par
\tab .[R] DIRECT\{ory\} /S [add /T for output to the system console]\page\f2\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f0\par
P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands.\par
\par
CA\{talog\}\cf1\i :u\par
\par
\cf0\i0\tab Obtain a quick TFS directory listing on the system console \tab printer.\par
\par
\tab Note: No storage device access is required unless the optional \cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  \tab is used; this forces the TFS directory to be read into memory \tab from the stated logical device unit; the TFS directory is \tab entirely cached during all keyboard monitor operations.\par
\par
  \tab The output format is four file names per line.  Since the maximum \tab number of files allowed is 84, the largest possible printout is\tab\tab 21 lines.\par
\par
\tab Note: Allocation of storage for files and system programs varies. \tab The P?S/8 Version 8P release for TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56, as used \tab with standard-length DECtapes, allows up to 72 files.  Longer \tab DECtapes will allow additional files up to the maximum of 84 \tab files.\par
\par
\tab Files are displayed in physical order of storage on the current \tab logical device unit.  Empty entries show as \cf4\highlight1 <FREE>\cf0\highlight0  and will \tab always be indicated unless physically last in the directory [and \tab thus the first of an indefinite number of inactive directory \tab slots not shown].\par
\par
\tab Note: Multiple EN\{ter\} and DE\{lete\} command operations can lead \tab to displaying a series of trailing empty entries in unusual \tab circumstances.  The CA\{talog\} command will display deleted file \tab entries as empty entries; only the very last entry [or the first \tab entry never used] is completely empty in the sense of either \tab having never been used, or cleared as a special case handled by \tab the DE\{lete\} command.  Trailing completely empty entries are not \tab displayed.  For more information regarding empty directory \tab entries, see the description of the DE\{lete\} command.\par
\par
DE\{lete\}\cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  \cf1\i filename:u\cf0\i0\par
\par
\tab Delete a file from the TFS directory.  The affected file name is \tab removed from the TFS directory; the underlying file contents are \tab not affected, making it possible to restore the file if the \tab deletion was unintended.  See Note 1.\par
\par
\tab Note: When a file is deleted from the TFS directory, the free \tab directory slot is available for storage of another file; if this \tab should occur, the original file is lost.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: The DE\{lete\} command does not check to potentially clear \tab the previous entry after clearing what becomes the first of \tab any final completely empty entries.  This is a deliberate design \tab decision to aid in file recovery; deleted entries generally \tab contain partial file names [unless the file name was only one or \tab two characters].  Appendix A contains information on recovering \tab from accidental file deletion using keyboard monitor commands and \tab specific system program utilities [if available].\par
\par
EC\{ho\} [optional 0 or 1]\par
\par
\tab Inverts the state of output of command characters if no argument \tab given.  EC\{ho\} 0 forces all output off; EC\{ho\} 1 forces all \tab output on.  Pressing \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 O\highlight0  during any output operation in \tab progress overrides this by immediately reversing the echo state \tab for each \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 O\highlight0  pressed.  This command is generally used \tab within BATCH control files to limit output to the system console.\par
\par
ED\{it\} [pre-existing line number]\par
\par
\tab ED\{it\} the contents of a specified line in the edit buffer.  The \tab ED\{it\} command is operated through the use of certain control \tab characters unique to the ED\{it\} command [as well as most of the \tab control characters that are used throughout the command \tab processor].\par
\par
\tab The operation of the ED\{it\} command is patterned after the FOCAL, \tab 1969 MO\{dify\} command with accommodations to the P?S/8 editing \tab environment; this includes features that apply to several \tab operating modes [including the automatic placement of line \tab numbers while entering text].  All control functions operate \tab consistently regardless of mode; additional control characters \tab also apply exclusively to the ED\{it\} command.  Description of \tab the applicable control characters follows:\par
\par
\tab 1)\tab Within the P?S/8 ED\{it\} command and the FOCAL, 1969 MO\{dify\} \tab\tab command, pressing \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 G\highlight0  initiates the search mode; the \tab\tab\tab next character typed is searched for from the current cursor \tab\tab position until either a matching character is found or the \tab\tab\tab end of the line is reached.\par
\par
\tab\tab In FOCAL, 1969, the MO\{dify\} operation starts as if \tab\tab\tab\tab\highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 G\highlight0  were already pressed; the P?S/8 ED\{it\} command \tab\tab\tab starts in character insertion mode after the line number and \tab\tab a \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  character are printed on the system console.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: For more information on the exact printing of the \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  \tab\tab character, see the description of the TA\{b\} command.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab At any point during the ED\{it\} command operation, all \tab\tab\tab\tab keyboard monitor functions are allowed including the \tab\tab\tab\tab insertion and deletion of text characters.  This can permit \tab\tab direct editing of the line number characters [and trailing \tab\tab\tab\highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  separator character] if desired.  It is possible to \tab\tab\tab create alternate line numbers with contents based on the \tab\tab\tab specified line number without changing the contents of the \tab\tab\tab specified line.  With the line number deleted, it is \tab\tab\tab\tab possible to create keyboard monitor commands to be executed \tab\tab in place based on the contents of the original line; this \tab\tab\tab facilitates using complex commands saved within text files \tab\tab\tab avoiding the need for manual entry for each usage.\tab\tab\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: It is also possible to create files containing a \tab\tab\tab single complex command; these files can be used with P?S/8 \tab\tab\tab BATCH without any form of editing.  By editing out all other \tab\tab lines in a file containing multiple complex commands, a \tab\tab\tab temporary file can be created containing only the desired \tab\tab\tab line; depending on the nature of the command, it may be \tab\tab\tab appropriate to save the file as one of the positional files \tab\tab $ or % due to their shorter access time.\par
\par
\tab\tab In FOCAL, 1969, the MO\{dify\} command will completely process \tab\tab and close out the specified line if the search does not \tab\tab\tab match; when this is unexpected, it can be quite disruptive \tab\tab\tab to the editing process.  Additional MO\{dify\} or other \tab\tab\tab\tab commands will be needed to resolve unforeseen issues.\par
 \par
\tab\tab In the P?S/8 ED\{it\} command, a failing search merely \tab\tab\tab\tab processes and outputs all the rest of the characters in the \tab\tab specified line [if any] and waits for further input [which \tab\tab\tab could include various abort or restart operations thus \tab\tab\tab preserving the original contents of the specified line].\par
\par
\tab 2)\tab In FOCAL, 1969, \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 L\highlight0  is used to search for the same \tab\tab\tab character as used in previous searches.  In the ED\{it\} \tab\tab\tab command, \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 A\highlight0  is used to search for the same character \tab\tab as used in previous searches.  Note: If no prior searches \tab\tab\tab have been performed, \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 A\highlight0  will output the rest of the \tab\tab\tab line contents [if any] to the system console terminal and \tab\tab\tab wait for futher input.  See Note 2.\par
\par
\tab 3)\tab In FOCAL, 1969, \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 J\highlight0  [\highlight3 <LF>\highlight0 ] processes and prints out \tab\tab\tab all remaining characters in the line on the system console \tab\tab\tab terminal; additional editing can be performed ending with \tab\tab\tab\highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 M\highlight0  [\highlight3 <CR>\highlight0 ] to close out the line.  See Note 2.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab Within the ED\{it\} command, \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 J\highlight0  [\highlight3 <LF>\highlight0 ] will output \tab\tab\tab\highlight3 <CR>\highlight0  and \highlight3 <LF>\highlight0  and then the current tentative line contents \tab\tab\tab to the system console terminal.  This is especially useful \tab\tab\tab throughout the keyboard monitor when using hard-copy console \tab\tab terminals which generally require the display of deleted \tab\tab\tab characters [which can be confusing].\par
\par
\tab 4)\tab\highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 U\highlight0  will completely restart the Ed\{it\} operation; \tab\tab\tab this includes the printing of the opening line number and \tab\tab\tab\highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  characters on the system console terminal.  No changes \tab\tab have been made to the specified line.\cf1\par
\par
\cf0\tab 5)\tab When FOCAL, 1969 was designed, the displayed character \tab\tab\tab associated with ASCII code 95 decimal [137 octal] [5F hex] \tab\tab\tab was implemented on early Teletype Model 33 and similar \tab\tab\tab console terminals as \cf2\highlight1\f2\fs29\u8592?\cf0\highlight0\f0\fs24 ; the displayed character was later \tab\tab\tab phased out of the ASCII character set and replaced with \cf2\highlight1\f2 _\cf0\highlight0\f0 .  \tab\tab The FOCAL, 1969 MO\{dify\} command uses this key to delete all \tab\tab text already output to the system console.\par
\par
\tab\tab It is apparent this function would have been assigned to an \tab\tab alternate code had the newer displayed character been the \tab\tab\tab usual graphic at the time; most newer software considers \tab\tab\tab this code equivalent to the backspace or delete key as \tab\tab\tab implemented on newer keyboards.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab Within the ED\{it\} command, \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 K\highlight0  will delete all \tab\tab\tab\tab characters already processed and output to the system \tab\tab\tab\tab console terminal.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: If the ED\{it\} command has not modified the line \tab\tab\tab\tab number, the line number and \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  will be preserved and will \tab\tab output to the system console terminal to continue the \tab\tab\tab  \tab editing process.  If the line number has already been \tab\tab\tab\tab modified or deleted, it will not be restored after\par
\tab\tab\highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 K\highlight0  has deleted all processed line contents.  If \tab\tab\tab\highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 U\highlight0  is used to restart the ED\{it\} command, the line \tab\tab\tab number [and \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0 ] will be restored as described in 4) above.\par
\cf1\par
\cf0\tab 6)\tab Pressing \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 H\highlight0  [\highlight3 <BS>\highlight0 ] initiates the \i rubout\i0  [delete \tab\tab\tab previous character in the line edit buffer] function.  This \tab\tab can also be accomplished using the DEL [\highlight3 <RO>\highlight0 ] key or \cf2\highlight1\f2 _\cf0\highlight0\f0  \tab\tab\tab [originally displayed as \cf2\highlight1\f2\fs29\u8592?\cf0\highlight0\f0\fs24  on certain older console \tab\tab\tab\tab terminals].\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab The specific implementation of this function is determined \tab\tab\tab by several keyboard monitor settings.  For most hard-copy \tab\tab\tab terminals, each instance echoes the previous character as it \tab\tab is deleted; this basic form of \i rubout\i0  handling can be \tab\tab\tab\tab implemented using the VARY RUBOUT ECHO command.  If the VARY \tab\tab RUBOUT TOPS10 command is used, all deleted characters will \tab\tab\tab be echoed in reverse order of deletion surrounded by \cf2\highlight1\f2\\\cf0\highlight0\f0  \tab\tab\tab characters.\par
\par
\tab\tab For display terminals, the most widely used method is to \tab\tab\tab output \highlight3 <BS>\highlight0  \highlight3 <SP>\highlight0  \highlight3 <BS>\highlight0  which visually obliterates the deleted \tab\tab character; this can be implemented using the VARY RUBOUT \tab\tab\tab BACKSP command.  Additional commands are available for \tab\tab\tab custom support of common DEC terminals such as the VT05, \tab\tab\tab VT52 and VT100.  For more information use the VARY /A \tab\tab\tab\tab command for a complete list of terminal customizations and \tab\tab\tab related issues.\par
\par
EN\{ter\}\cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  \cf1\i filename:u\cf0\i0\par
\par
\tab A specified [or default from a prior file name command] file name \tab is entered into the directory; no actual file storage is \tab modified.\par
\par
\tab Appendix A contains information on recovering from accidental \tab file deletion using the EN\{ter\} and other keyboard monitor \tab commands along with specific system program utilities [if \tab available].\par
\par
\tab Judicious use of the EN\{ter\} command can place files for maximum \tab efficiency.  On certain tape systems, some programs can access \tab the contents of files faster if the files are arranged in an \tab interleaved manner; the EN\{ter\} command can setup the proper file \tab placement prior to use.\par
\par
\tab For example, on TC01/TC08/TC55/TU56 systems, it is impossible to \tab access adjacent files without causing a tape turnaround.\par
\par
\tab Note: This is a hardware restriction.  If a DECtape handler \tab allowed tape motion when the handler exits, the tape would always \tab eventually wind into the forward end zone; the next tape \tab operation would first have to rewind the tape to just below the\par
\tab desired initial tape block, then turn around to cause forward \tab tape motion.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab Since every call to the system device handler stops the tape upon \tab exit, the next call must be to an area slightly further up the \tab tape yet not immediately adjacent for a forward initial search to \tab be effective.  In the specific case of TFS files, this means \tab attempts to access the immediately adjacent file will cause a \tab tape turnaround; accessing a file an additional file slot [or \tab more] further up the tape will start with forward motion and the \tab tape operation will successfully finish without a tape \tab turnaround.\par
\par
\tab Thus, by arranging the files of a project in an interleaved \tab order, access to the files is greatly increased.  The interleave \tab can be accomplished either by having files from an unrelated \tab project interdigitated with the current project files, or the \tab project files can be split into halves, with the second half \tab files between the first half files; there would be no turnarounds \tab in the former case and a single turnaround in the latter case due \tab to having to rewind to access the second half files.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab The \i slurp\i0  binary loader for TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 always starts \tab with an initial forward search to more quickly access the binary \tab files, which are generally located further up the tape from the \tab loader code image.  The interleaved file approach will decrease \tab file access time for binary files as this further improves the \tab loading process; as such, it would be prudent to interleave \tab binary output files with the source code files of the same \tab project.  Both sets of files will be accessed in an optimal way \tab by the programs generally associated with these files \tab [respectively PAL for source code files and the BIN binary file \tab loader for \i slurp\i0  binary files].  See Note 3.\par
\par
HA\{lt\}\par
\par
\tab The computer deliberately stops running.  Press \cf2\highlight1\f2 Continue\cf0\highlight0\f0  [or \tab equivalent if available] to restart the machine; the keyboard \tab monitor continues in the same state it was prior to the machine \tab halting.\par
\par
\tab Do not use this command on systems that do not support the \tab\cf2\highlight1\f2 Continue\cf0\highlight0\f0  functionality; the only remedy would be to perform a \tab system restart which would reload the keyboard monitor without \tab saving the edit buffer or other current particulars.\par
\par
\tab Note: It is merely a vicious and unfounded rumor that the \tab command also causes the machine to catch fire.  The PDP-8 \tab supports the HLT instruction to stop program execution; it is the \tab stuff of legend there is the HCF instruction to both Halt and \tab Catch Fire; P?S/8 stops the processor using the HLT instruction\par
\tab which would tend to cool the computer.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
HR\{eader\} [\i optional-starting-line-number\i0 ] [\i optional-line-increment\i0 ]\par
\par
\tab Auto-sequenced input is initiated starting at the specified\par
\tab\i optional-starting-line-number\i0  [line 100 if none given]. \tab Subsequent lines are incremented by the \i optional-line-increment\i0  \tab value [default is 10 if none given].  If available, the input \tab characters are read from the high-speed paper-tape reader; \tab otherwise, this command is identical to the SE\{quence\} command.\par
\par
\tab Auto-sequence mode can be terminated by \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 D\highlight0 .  The standard \tab P?S/8 text character set applies; lower-case characters will be \tab "folded" to the upper-case equivalent.  See Note 4.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
LA\{st\}\par
\par
\tab This command outputs the line number of the highest existing line \tab [if any] to the system console terminal.  All line numbers must \tab be in the range of 0 through 4094 decimal [0 through 7776 octal].\par
\par
\tab Note: Perverse resequencing operations can cause an overflow \tab condition.  If required, the situation can always be rectified \tab without file loss by using smaller line number increments in a \tab subsequent resequence operation [such as merely using the default \tab values].  If the user has some familiarity with the contents of a \tab particular file, the LA\{st\} command can confirm the highest line \tab number is in range; if the line numbers overflow, listing the \tab file contents will show lack of proper line number order.  After \tab a valid resequencing operation, the file will again show ordered \tab line numbers; the LA\{st\} command will output the highest line \tab number as expected.\par
\par
\tab Note: In all releases of P?S/8, resequence operations perform an \tab automatic LA\{st\} command; if the user is familiar with the file \tab contents, the effects of line number overflow will be immediately\par
\tab evident.\par
\par
LE\{ft\}\par
\par
\tab The free word count within the edit buffer is shown on the \tab system console terminal.  See Note 5.\par
\par
\tab As more text lines are added to the edit buffer, the free word \tab count decreases.  A full file condition occurs when there is \tab insufficient free space to insert the latest line contained in \tab the command-line buffer; the \cf2\highlight1\f2 FILE FULL\cf0\highlight0\f0  error message is shown on \tab the system console terminal and the latest command-line contents \tab are lost.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
\par
\cf0\tab The LE\{ft\} command can serve as a warning mechanism indicating \tab how much free space is currently available.  If a file is nearly \tab full, corrective remedies can be applied [such as splitting the \tab file into two independent files as detailed in the description of \tab the SC\{ratch\} command].\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab All TFS files are 2048 12-bit words [4096 six-bit bytes] long \tab regardless of internal content.  Within TFS text files, two 12-\tab bit \tab words are used for line number and line pointer purposes for \tab each file line.  All lines start on 12-bit word boundaries.  \tab Since each line has its own line pointer, all file modifications \tab are performed nearly instantaneously.\par
\par
  \tab While the line pointers and associated line numbers of all file \tab lines are 12-bit words, the rest of the file predominantly \tab consists of six-bit bytes; lines that end with an odd byte count \tab include a filler byte to maintain 12-bit word alignment.  All \tab file lines are contiguously stored with the line pointer and \tab line number values stored at the back end of the file; there is a \tab single 12-bit word set to zero after the last file line to serve \tab as end of file for all programs that only read text characters.\par
\par
\tab There is a four 12-bit word reserved area; as such, the LE\{ft\} \tab command will report 2044 words available in an empty file.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab A good rule of thumb is to double the free word count to gauge \tab how many additional bytes can be added to the file.  It is \tab advisable to have a reasonable reserve within every file for \tab future editing purposes [thus avoiding loss of the command line \tab when attempting to add another line to the file].  Most users \tab should consider SA\{ving\} the edit buffer to a file when the \tab available space falls below 100 words; the edit buffer can then \tab be cleared with the SC\{ratch\} command to start the next file.  \tab For a typical\tab project, the contents of multiple files passed to \tab any system program are concatenated together as a continuous \tab input stream; a list of up to 17 input files may be passed to any \tab system program.\par
\par
LI\{st\} [\i line-number-range\i0 ]\par
\par
\tab All file lines in the edit buffer in the specified \i line-number-\tab range\i0  are output to the system console terminal.\par
\par
\tab Note: Output formatting is affected by the current horizontal tab \tab setting as each line of output starts with the line number \tab followed by \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0 .  This equally applies to additional \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  \tab characters within the file.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
LO\{ad\}\cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  \cf1\i filename:u\cf0\i0\par
\par
\tab A specified [or default from a prior file name command] file name \tab is located within the TFS directory; the file contents are loaded \tab into the edit buffer.  Keyboard monitor/integrated line editor \tab commands may be used to examine or modify the contents of the \tab edit buffer.  The original file is unchanged unless an explicit \tab SA\{ave\} command is performed.  See Note 6.\par
\par
\tab Note: Attempts to load nonexistent files will cause error \tab messages.\par
\par
MO\{nitor\}\par
\par
\tab Invoking the MO\{nitor\} command exits the keyboard monitor; the \tab system is rebooted by restarting the computer at 07600; this is \tab entirely equivalent to a manual restart at the same address.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: Using the MO\{nitor\} command in BATCH files may change the \tab overall flow of BATCH operations depending on specific BATCH \tab options chosen.  A BATCH file under development may include the \tab MO\{nitor\} command to temporarily limit the overall scope of the \tab BATCH file as necessary.  Additional information about the P?S/8 \tab BATCH utility is available in other documents.\par
\par
NU\{mber\} [\i optional-starting-line-number\i0 ] [\i optional-line-increment\i0 ]\par
\par
\tab Auto-sequenced input is initiated starting at the specified\par
\tab\i optional-starting-line-number\i0  [line 100 if none given].  \tab Subsequent lines are incremented by the \i optional-line-increment\i0  \tab value [default is 10 if none given].  See Note 7.\par
\par
\tab During text entry, it is possible to delete all or a portion of \tab the sequence number to execute another command [or perhaps change \tab the line number that applies].  Auto-sequence mode can be \tab terminated by pressing \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 D\highlight0 .  The standard P?S/8 text \tab character set applies; lower-case characters will be "folded" to \tab the upper-case equivalent.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\cf0\par
\par
PL\{ease\} [character string]\par
\par
\tab Executing the PL\{ease\} command will display the entire command \tab line [including any character string past the command] on the \tab system console terminal.  All operations are paused until any \tab non-control character is pressed [which will otherwise be \tab ignored].\par
\par
\tab A typical usage of the PL\{ease\} command is within a BATCH file to \tab allow some external action to be applied:\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab .^^ GET PROGRAM LISTING BATCH CONTROL FILE.\par
\tab .^^\par
\tab .^^ LAST EDIT: 07-APR-2015 CJL\par
\tab .^^\par
\tab .PLEASE TURN ON THE LINE PRINTER OR PRESS CONTROL-C TO ABORT ...\par
\cf1\tab [A character is pressed by the operator which is not echoed.]\cf0\par
\tab .^^\par
\tab .^^ THANK YOU FOR ENABLING THE LINE PRINTER; CONTINUING ...\par
\par
\tab Certain P?S/8 programs output to the system line printer [if \tab available] otherwise falling back on the system console terminal.\tab [Some programs have command-line options to prevent discovery of \tab the line printer which is the default action.]  By inserting \tab appropriate lines into the BATCH file similar to the above \tab example, the BATCH can be run as intended.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: All of the active control characters are monitored for \tab their normal function during the command pause initiated by the\tab\tab PL\{ease\} command; the system remains paused until another non-\tab control character is pressed.\par
\par
\tab Tip: The PL\{ease\} command can cause the system to be paused \tab while character echo is off.  When designing BATCH scripts, it is \tab recommended the control file output appropriate messages before\par
 \tab and after executing the PL\{ease\} command to prevent confusion.\par
\par
PU\{nch\} [\i line-number-range\i0 ]\par
\par
\tab This command is similar to the LI\{st\} command except the output \tab is directed to the high-speed paper-tape punch instead of the \tab console terminal; the line numbers [and associated \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  \tab characters] are suppressed.  Punched output consists of 128 \tab standard leader frames [high-bit only set] followed by the \tab contents of the file lines in the specified \i line-number-range\i0  \tab then 128 additional standard trailer frames.  See Note 4.\par
\par
\tab Note: In some P?S/8 releases, if the high-speed paper-tape punch \tab is not available, the output is directed to the system console \tab terminal.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
R \i system-program\cf1 :u\cf0\i0  [options]\par
\par
\tab Load the specified \i system-program\i0  initial segment into memory and \tab start at the starting address indicated in the system  programs \tab directory.  Various optional parameters are allowed:\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab 1)\tab Files.\par
\par
\tab\tab An optional list of output files or input files [or both] \tab\tab\tab can be passed to a system program.  Output files [if any] \tab\tab\tab must precede input files [if any].  The maximum number of \tab\tab\tab files is 17.  Output files are indicated by \cf2\highlight1\f2 <\cf0\highlight0\f0  after the last \tab\tab output file specification; input files [if any] follow \tab\tab\tab output files [if present].\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: Specific system programs may further restrict the \tab\tab\tab maximum passed file count.\par
\par
\tab 2)\tab =\i xxxx\i0  parameter.\par
\par
\tab\tab This is a numerical value generally expressed in octal in \tab\tab\tab the range of 0 through 7776 [0 through 4094 decimal].  While \tab\tab 7777 [4095 decimal] cannot be expressed directly, this value \tab\tab is used by default when the =\i xxxx\i0  is not used.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: Certain system programs allow the use of the current \tab\tab\tab radix in effect for the =\i xxxx\i0  parameter; most programs force \tab\tab octal radix.\par
\par
\tab 3)\tab Option switches.\par
\par
\tab\tab The option switches include \cf2\highlight1\f2 /A\cf0\highlight0\f0  through \cf2\highlight1\f2 /Z\cf0\highlight0\f0  and \cf2\highlight1\f2 /0\cf0\highlight0\f0  through \cf2\highlight1\f2 /9\cf0\highlight0\f0 .  \tab\tab When expressing switches in groups, the individual \cf2\highlight1\f2 /\cf0\highlight0\f0  is \tab\tab\tab replaced with a leading \cf2\highlight1\f2 (\cf0\highlight0\f0  followed by one or more option \tab\tab\tab switches, each expressed as a single character, followed by \tab\tab an optional trailing \cf2\highlight1\f2 )\cf0\highlight0\f0 .\par
\par
\tab All options can be included at any point after the system program \tab name; all values [other than file name lists] can be specified \tab redundantly without error.  \highlight3 <SP>\highlight0  and \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  characters are freely \tab allowed between values to clarify the command formation if \tab desired.\par
\par
\tab To maintain backward compatibility with the R-L Monitor System,\par
\tab\cf2\highlight1\f2 ,\cf0\highlight0\f0  is allowed as an alternate file name separator [with or without \tab additional \highlight3 <SP>\highlight0  or \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  characters].  Input file lists may be \tab preceded with \cf2\highlight1\f2 <\cf0\highlight0\f0  even if no output files are specified.\par
\par
\tab The position-optimized files % and $ are freely allowed as input \tab [or output] files.  New output files will be automatically \tab entered into the TFS directory.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab Logical unit considerations.\par
\par
\tab The default logical unit for file names is the current logical \tab unit in effect.  This may have been changed from the bootup \tab logical unit by commands issued prior to the R command.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab The default logical unit for the \i system-program\i0  is the bootup \tab logical unit.\par
\par
\tab Both \i system-program\i0  and file specifications can override the \tab prevailing default logical units using explicit \cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  constructs as \tab is the case in various other keyboard monitor commands.  Explicit \tab logical unit changes in file specifications update the default \tab value for any additional file specifications on the command line.\par
\par
\tab By design, all output files are processed before all input files.\par
\par
\tab Concise program execution.\par
\par
\tab When the first two characters of the \i system-program\i0  name are not \tab in conflict with keyboard monitor commands, the \i system-program\i0  \tab may be invoked directly [including all options].  Depending on \tab the P?S/8 system release, this may cause the command process to \tab commence sooner [due to lower internal overhead]; in certain \tab instances, concise program execution may be the only constructive \tab way to use the program due to dependence on the availability of \tab the edit buffer [which is not preserved when the \i system-program\i0  \tab is invoked by the R command].\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab Extended-length file issues.\par
\par
\tab Most P?S/8 system programs are capable of reading the textual \tab contents of extended-length files created by conversion utilities \tab designed for the specific intersystem process.  In the case of \tab the BIGCON utility program, OS/8-format data is read from one \tab DECtape while an extended-length file is created on another.\par
\par
\tab When P?S/8 Version 8P was released circa 1976, the directory \tab structure for extended-length files was not yet fully defined.  \tab As such, extended-length file creation is a very crude and error-\tab prone process.\par
\par
\tab Instead of a properly-managed block defined in an extended-length \tab files directory, the user designates a standard TFS file's first \tab block as the starting point for writing out extended-length file \tab data.  As such, all blocks present on the system device past this \tab file are in danger of being overwritten; the file chosen must be \tab the highest one present in the TFS directory for the conversion \tab to be successful.  See Note 8.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab The upper limit block for writing out the extended-length file is \tab determined by reading the front panel switch register.  Since the \tab only known surviving Version 8P system is for TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 \tab DECtape, a standard DECtape will be used in the example below \tab regarding using the \i BIGCON\i0  utility:\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab Since the highest block on a standard DECtape is 2701, the switch \tab register should be set to 2677, the highest block for a tape size \tab of 2700 blocks.  [Due to internal buffering issues within the \tab BIGCON utility, the upper limit block should be for a device size \tab that is a multiple of eight blocks.]\par
\par
\tab Assuming the first file present in the TFS directory is the only \tab one in the directory, the starting block should be 0500 or \tab thereabouts.  The following P?S/8 command session illustrates a \tab successful conversion.\par
\par
Extended-length output file conversion example.\par
\par
Console output\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\cf3\f2 Comments\par
\cf0\f0\par
.CA\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\cf3\f2 CA[talog\} command.\cf0\f0\par
LGFILE\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\cf3\f2 Contents of TFS directory.\cf0\f0\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\cf3\f2 Highest file is LGFILE.\cf0\f0\par
.ODT LGFILE\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\cf3\f2 Optional ODT check to\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab confirm file block.\cf0\f0\par
7757/0500 E\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\cf3\f2 File block is 0500; exit.\cf0\f0\par
.R BIGCON,LGFILE</1=7\tab\tab\tab\tab\cf3\f2 OS/8 data on drive 1.\cf0\f0\par
.\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\cf3\f2 Conversion complete.\cf1\f0\par
\par
\cf0 The LGFILE now extends from block 0500 through some undetermined block less than 2700.\par
\par
This file can be used as valid input to programs such as the PAL assembler, which ignores file line numbers [if present].  The entire text of the converted OS/8 file can be referenced by the single file name LGFILE.\par
\par
The 1976 release of P?S/8 includes the \i CONVRT\i0  program to convert the contents of TFS files to/from OS/8 data.  The BIGCON program can also be used to convert the contents of P?S/8 files to OS/8 data; the OS/8 data can be converted to extended-length files as described above.\par
\par
Later releases of P?S/8 replaced both programs with the \i OS8CON\i0  utility that can convert all the data file variants mentioned; command-line options switches determine the type and direction of the conversion.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
