RA\{dix\} [8 or 10]\par
\par
\tab When the RA\{dix\} command is invoked without an optional \tab parameter, the radix for all numerical input or output is \tab reversed to the opposite of the current value in effect.  This \tab applies to all contexts where a numerical value may be expressed \tab including file line numbers, passing parameters to programs using \tab the =\i xxxx\i0  construct [under certain circumstances] and the \tab numerical output of commands such as LA\{st\} and LE\{ft\}.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab The radix can be set by passing an explicit parameter: RA\{dix\} 10 \tab will set the radix to decimal; RA\{dix\} 8 will set the radix to \tab octal.\par
\par
\tab Note: The current radix does not affect the interpretation of the \tab passed parameter; the current radix is saved and then set \tab internally to decimal.  If the command fails due to improper \tab input, the previous radix is restored.\par
\par
\tab Changes made with the RA[dix] command revert to the system \tab default value upon system restart; the scope of the RA\{dix\} \tab command is within the current keyboard monitor session only.  \tab While the usual overall default is decimal, this may be changed \tab using the VARY RADIX EIGHT command.\par
\par
\tab The radix chosen for most work is a user preference setting.  \tab Certain limited examples of octal output may be useful to explain \tab basic PDP-8 assembly concepts as illustrated below.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab .RADIX 8\par
\pard\tx1260\tx2520\tx3780\tx4860\tx6120\par
     .SEQUENCE 177 1\par
\par
      177            *200                    /GET THE DESIRED ORIGIN.\par
      200    BEGIN,  TAD     (7)             /GET A <BEL> CHARACTER.\par
      201            TLS                     /RING IT.\par
      202            JMP I   (7600)          /RESTART THE SYSTEM NOW.\par
\par
\pard\tab Note: The above example is not an actual assembly but it does \tab cosmetically resemble a simplified assembly listing.  Beginners \tab can learn certain practical aspects of assembly language before \tab mastering additional concepts needed to carry out assembly \tab language programming on a more complete basis.  Since the line \tab numbers are permanent [assuming the edit buffer is SA\{ved\} to a \tab file], small changes can be made which will reflect the way the \tab program actually loads into memory.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
RE\{sequence\} [\i optional-starting-line-number\i0 ] [\i optional-line-increment\i0 ]\par
\par
\tab The RE[sequence] command reorders the line numbers within the \tab edit buffer as specified by the \i optional-starting-line-number\i0  and\par
\i\tab optional-line-increment\i0  accordingly.  After the resequencing \tab operation [which is carried out virtually instantly], all file \tab lines will be in equal increment order.  Note: No changes are \tab made to the text contents of nor the relative order of the lines; \tab however, all future references to the lines must be made with the \tab new line number values.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab Resequencing is required when a section of a file has been \tab heavily modified adding in lines with numeric values in between \tab original lines.  This can leave little room for additional lines \tab within the section.  By setting relatively large line increments, \tab it is possible to set aside sufficient numerical space in the \tab file for the purpose of adding lines [typically with an increment \tab of 1]. Depending on the scope of the editing operation, it may be \tab necessary to resequence the file more than once.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab Tip: Assume a file is sequenced with an increment of 10.  There \tab is the possibility of adding in lines 101 through 109 between \tab line 100 and line 110.  The ED[it] command can be used to create \tab a line 129 from the contents of line 120 [which can then be \tab deleted].  By changing one line, there is now the possibility of \tab adding in lines 101 through 128.  If the file is resequenced [or \tab was created with even larger increments] then more than enough \tab line number space can be available for any reasonable edit \tab session.\par
\par
\tab Note: In certain editing situations, the limiting factor may be \tab file capacity; it may be necessary to first split the file into \tab two independent files before applying localized changes.  For \tab more information regarding file splitting, see the description of \tab the SC\{ratch\} command.\par
\par
\tab Once a file is stable, it is always recommended to perform a \tab final resequence operation to the default values.\par
\par
\tab After the resequencing, an automatic LA[st] command is performed.  \tab All line numbers must be in the range of 0 through 4094 [0 \tab through 7776 in octal radix].\par
\par
\tab Note: It is possible to overflow the line number range by either \tab using a starting line number that is too high or a line increment \tab that is too large; this can always be remedied by using smaller \tab parameters such as the default values of starting at line 100 \tab with a line increment of 10.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
RU\{n\} \i system-program\cf1 :u\cf0\i0  [options]\par
\par
\tab The RU\{n\} command is equivalent to the R command; all\par
\tab parameter considerations are identical in both commands.  See \tab Note 8.\par
\par
SA\{ve\}\cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  \cf1\i filename:u\cf0\i0\par
\par
\tab The edit buffer contents will be saved in the stated [or implied \tab from a prior file name usage] file slot.  The TFS directory will \tab be updated first to allocate a new file slot if necessary; if the \tab slot exists, the file will be written out directly.  See Note 9.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: On systems based on DECtape or other serial access devices, \tab the time savings when updating a file can be profound.\par
\par
SC\{ratch\} [\i line-number-range\i0 ]\par
\par
\tab All lines in the specified \i line-number-range\i0  are removed from the \tab edit buffer.  See Note 10.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab The SC\{ratch\} command is often used to split a file into two \tab smaller files when the file is nearly full.  A split-point line \tab number is determined to separate the contents of the current \tab file.\par
\par
\tab\highlight3\f2 Note: Novice users are advised to make a backup copy of the file\highlight0\par
\f0\tab\highlight3\f2 before attempting this to avoid data loss.\par
\highlight0\f0\par
\tab 1)\tab All lines from the split-point onward can be deleted by \tab\tab\tab using the command SC\{ratch\} XXXX- [where XXXX is the chosen \tab\tab split-point line number] which creates the smaller file \tab\tab\tab which consists of all lines up to the split-point.  The \tab\tab\tab edit buffer should be SA\{ved\} into the file designated to \tab\tab\tab contain the first part of the former file.\par
\par
\tab 2)\tab The complete [former] file should be reloaded using the\par
\tab\tab LO\{ad\} command; all lines from the beginning of the file \tab\tab\tab through the split-point should be deleted using the command \tab\tab SC\{ratch\} -YYYY [where YYYY is the value XXXX above less 1] \tab\tab which creates the smaller file which consists of all lines \tab\tab\tab beyond the split-point.  The edit buffer should be SA\{ved\} \tab\tab\tab into the file designated to contain the second part of the \tab\tab\tab former file.\par
\par
\tab Tip: Since the specific line number used in the ranges given to \tab the SC\{ratch\} command need not exist [and merely serves as a \tab limit], a line that is exactly one lower than the intended split-\tab point [and non-existent] can be used in both operations when \tab splitting the original file.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab Tip: The method illustrated is an example of one way to \tab accomplish the file-splitting operation.  By using equivalent \tab alternative steps, the same net result can be achieved by first \tab saving the ending portion of the original file then creating the \tab beginning portion.\par
\par
\tab Tip: Seasoned users will reuse the original file name as one of \tab the two descendant files; only one additional file need be \tab specified within the overall stream of project files; the \tab commands used to pass a stream of files to a system program [PAL, \tab FOCAL, etc.] should be updated accordingly.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab Observation: Many competing systems require full-file editing; \tab this means that sufficient space on the storage device is tied up \tab for roughly twice the size of the entire project at the point of \tab any editing operation; later the obsolete copy is released, \tab generally amounting to an inadequate smaller portion of the \tab storage device that eventually leads to serious fragmentation\par
\tab and overhead issues.  By limiting editing to fixed size portions \tab of the project, it is only necessary to periodically allocate an \tab additional file at a time to resolve line number problems by \tab splitting the file as described above.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: After repeated editing operations on a source file project, \tab certain files will tend to be nearly full while others may be \tab more sparsely populated.  This can be remedied by periodically \tab using the CONVRT program to create a single file in OS/8 format, \tab then use CONVRT to recreate the source files from the OS/8 format \tab data; CONVRT standardizes the usage of each file thus eliminating \tab problems of variation in file usage.  A report is given of how \tab many files were created; it is conceivable the repacked file set \tab could occupy less files total; however, in rare instances it is \tab conceivable that additional files might be needed due to the \tab standardization.  Additional files can be added to the list of \tab output files as necessary.  The CONVRT program will indicate how\tab\tab many files were actually used; the rest can be discarded.\par
\par
SE\{quence\} [\i optional-starting-line-number\i0 ] [\i optional-line-increment\i0 ]\par
\par
\tab Auto-sequenced input is initiated starting at the specified\par
\tab\i optional-starting-line-number\i0  [line 100 if none given].  \tab Subsequent lines are incremented by the \i optional-line-increment\i0  \tab value [default is 10 if none given].\par
\par
\tab During text entry, it is possible to delete all or a portion of \tab the sequence number to execute another command [or perhaps change \tab the line number that applies].  Auto-sequence mode can be \tab terminated by pressing \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 D\highlight0 .  The standard P?S/8 text \tab character set applies; lower-case characters will be "folded" to \tab the upper-case equivalent.  See Note 11.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
