SY\{stem\}\cf1\i :u\cf0\i0\par
\par
\tab The device logical unit \cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  specified in the SY\{stem\} command is \tab selected as the new logical unit for all subsequent file-oriented \tab logical unit purposes.  If the \cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  is omitted or the previous \tab logical unit and the new logical unit are identical, the command \tab is effectively ignored.  As required, the corresponding TFS \tab directory on the new logical unit is read into memory replacing \tab the previous one.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: For all operations of the keyboard monitor and integrated \tab editor, the TFS directory on the current logical unit is 100% \tab resident in memory; in many instances, this causes an \tab extraordinary decrease in file access time.\par
\par
\tab The SY\{stem\} command merely changes the logical unit without \tab otherwise performing another action.  See Note 12.\line\par
\tab Tip: If a series of operations must be performed on a particular \tab logical unit as specified within a BATCH file, the first \tab operation must effect a change to the intended logical unit.  \tab When a BATCH file is created, it is often not possible to \tab determine the intended logical unit until the actual \tab configuration is finalized.  The SY\{stem\} command can be used \tab to initialize the set of commands placed further into the BATCH \tab file stream [all without local logical unit settings] along with \tab other appropriate initialization such as a series of console \tab messages or PL\{ease\} commands as required.  The SY\{stem\} \tab command can be located in a separate initialization file to \tab modularize the components of the BATCH operation; as many as 17 \tab control files can be passed at one time to BATCH.\par
\par
TA\{b\} [0 3 5]\par
\par
\tab The printing disposition of the \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  character within the \tab keyboard monitor can be customized in various ways to achieve the \tab usual DEC-style alignment to the nearest eight spaces.  All \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  \tab characters are affected such as the printed separation between \tab the line number and the file line text, or any \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  characters \tab within the text.\par
\par
\tab The \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  character can be displayed in one of four operating \tab modes:\par
\par
\tab 1) \cf2\highlight1\f2 TA\cf0\highlight0\f0\tab The \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  is printed as an actual \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  character; the \tab\tab\tab\tab system console terminal must support DEC-style tab \tab\tab\tab\tab stops every eight columns.  This is the most efficient \tab\tab\tab operating mode assuming there is hardware support for \tab\tab\tab\tab the <HT> character; this is generally true on most \tab\tab\tab\tab modern terminals or terminal emulators.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1976 [Version 8P] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab 2) \cf2\highlight1\f2 TA 5\cf0\highlight0\f0\tab This mode is similar to 1) above except \highlight3 <NUL>\highlight0  \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab characters are used as filler characters as required \tab\tab\tab\tab for custom support of the Teletype Model 35.  The exact \tab\tab\tab number of filler characters required is dependent on \tab\tab\tab\tab the relative position of the carriage within the latest \tab\tab\tab eight-position tab stop.  For certain positions, two \tab\tab\tab\tab filler characters are required; for some other \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab positions one filler is sufficient.  For two specific\par
\tab\tab\tab positions, no filler is required.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab\tab Note: This is the proper method to handle this terminal \tab\tab\tab device; in certain software systems, the Teletype Model \tab\tab\tab 35 is incorrectly handled by always using a single \tab\tab\tab\tab filler character.  This is a needless print delay in \tab\tab\tab\tab some instances and inadequate wait time in other \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab situations which will cause printhead ink smearing.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab 3) \cf2\highlight1\f2 TA 3\cf0\highlight0\f0\tab This mode simulates horizontal tab stops by outputting \tab\tab\tab the proper number of \highlight3 <SP>\highlight0  characters to maintain \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab alignment; this is required on printing terminals such \tab\tab\tab as the Teletype Model 33 with no actual horizontal tab \tab\tab\tab support.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab This is the slowest operating mode with the single \tab\tab\tab\tab advantage of universally functioning on all console \tab\tab\tab\tab devices.\par
\par
\tab 4) \cf2\highlight1\f2 TA 0\cf0\highlight0\f0\tab The \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  character is printed as one space character; \tab\tab\tab\tab this is an option for very slow hard-copy terminals \tab\tab\tab\tab where the trade-off of print time versus cosmetics may \tab\tab\tab be a factor.\par
\par
\tab The TA\{b\} command allows temporary override of the permanent \tab setting for \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  printing.  Users of slow terminals may opt to \tab change the operating mode from \cf2\highlight1\f2 TA 3\cf0\highlight0\f0  to \cf2\highlight1\f2 TA 0\cf0\highlight0\f0  to more quickly \tab obtain a minimal listing.\par
\par
\tab The system default setting is TA\{b\} 3 for maximum compatibility \tab with most terminals.  This can be modified using an appropriate \tab sub-option of the system VARY utility TTY option.\par
