CA\{talog\}\cf1\i :u\par
\par
\cf0\i0\tab Obtain a quick TFS directory listing on the system console \tab printer.\par
\par
\tab Note: No storage device access is required unless the optional \cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  \tab is used; this forces the TFS directory to be read into memory \tab from the stated logical device unit; the TFS directory is \tab entirely cached during all keyboard monitor operations.\par
\par
\tab The output format is four file names per line.  Since the maximum \tab number of files allowed is 84, the largest possible printout is\tab\tab 21 lines.\par
\par
\tab Note: Allocation of storage for files and system programs varies. \tab The P?S/8 Version 8Z release for TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56, as used \tab with standard-length DECtapes, allows up to 60 files.  Longer \tab DECtapes will allow additional files up to the maximum of 84 \tab files.\par
\par
\tab Files are displayed in physical order of storage on the current \tab logical device unit.  Empty entries show as \cf4\highlight1 <FREE>\cf0\highlight0  and will \tab always be indicated unless physically last in the directory [and \tab thus the first of an indefinite number of inactive directory \tab slots not shown].\par
\par
\tab Note: Multiple EN\{ter\} and KI\{ll\} command operations can lead to \tab displaying a series of trailing empty entries in unusual \tab circumstances.  The CA\{talog\} command will print out deleted file \tab entries as empty entries; only the very last entry [or the first \tab entry never used] is completely empty in the sense of either \tab having never been used, or cleared as a special case handled by \tab the KI\{ll\} command.  Trailing completely empty entries are not \tab displayed.  For more information regarding empty directory \tab entries, see the description of the KI\{ll\} command.\par
\par
 EC\{ho\} [optional 0 or 1]\par
\par
\tab Inverts the state of output of command characters if no argument \tab given.  EC\{ho\} 0 forces all output off; EC\{ho\} 1 forces all \tab output on.  Pressing \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 O\highlight0  during any output operation in \tab progress overrides this by immediately reversing the echo state \tab for each \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 O\highlight0  pressed.  This command is generally used \tab within BATCH control files to limit output to the system console.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
