ED\{it\} [pre-existing line number]\par
\par
\tab ED\{it\} the contents of a specified line in the edit buffer.  The \tab ED\{it\} command is operated through the use of certain control \tab characters unique to the ED\{it\} command [as well as most of the \tab control characters that are used throughout the command \tab processor].\par
\par
\tab The operation of the ED\{it\} command is patterned after the FOCAL, \tab 1969 MO\{dify\} command with accommodations to the P?S/8 editing \tab environment; this includes features that apply to several \tab operating modes [including the automatic placement of line \tab numbers while entering text].  All control functions operate \tab consistently regardless of mode; additional control characters \tab also apply exclusively to the ED\{it\} command.  Description of \tab the applicable control characters follows:\par
\par
\tab 1)\tab Within the P?S/8 ED\{it\} command and the FOCAL, 1969 MO\{dify\} \tab\tab command, pressing \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 G\highlight0  initiates the search mode; the \tab\tab\tab next character typed is searched for from the current cursor \tab\tab position until either a matching character is found or the \tab\tab\tab end of the line is reached.\par
\par
\tab\tab In FOCAL, 1969, the MO\{dify\} operation starts as if \tab\tab\tab\tab\highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 G\highlight0  were already pressed; the P?S/8 ED\{it\} command \tab\tab\tab starts in character insertion mode after the line number and \tab\tab a \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  character are printed on the system console.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: For more information on the exact printing of the \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  \tab\tab character, see the description of the TA\{b\} command.\par
\par
\tab\tab At any point during the ED\{it\} command operation, all \tab\tab\tab\tab keyboard monitor functions are allowed including the \tab\tab\tab\tab insertion and deletion of text characters.  This can effect \tab\tab direct editing of the line number characters [and trailing \tab\tab\tab\highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  separator character] if desired.  It is possible to \tab\tab\tab create alternate line numbers with contents based on the \tab\tab\tab specified line number without changing the contents of the \tab\tab\tab specified line.  With the line number deleted, it is \tab\tab\tab\tab possible to create keyboard monitor commands to be executed \tab\tab in place based on the contents of the original line; this \tab\tab\tab facilitates using complex commands saved within text files \tab\tab\tab avoiding the need for manual entry for each usage.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab Note: It is also possible to create files containing a \tab\tab\tab single complex command; these files can be used with P?S/8 \tab\tab\tab BATCH without any form of editing.  By editing out all other \tab\tab lines in a file containing multiple complex commands, a \tab\tab\tab temporary file can be created containing only the desired \tab\tab\tab line; depending on the nature of the command, it may be \tab\tab\tab appropriate to save the file as one of the positional files \tab\tab $ or % due to their shorter access time.\par
\par
\tab\tab In FOCAL, 1969, the MO\{dify\} command will completely process \tab\tab and close out the specified line if the search does not \tab\tab\tab match; when this is unexpected, it can be quite disruptive \tab\tab\tab to the editing process.  Additional MO\{dify] or other \tab\tab\tab\tab commands will be needed to resolve unforeseen issues.\par
\par
\tab\tab Within the P?S/8 ED\{it\} command, a failing search merely \tab\tab\tab processes and outputs all the rest of the characters in the \tab\tab specified line [if any] and waits for further input [which \tab\tab\tab could include various abort or restart operations thus \tab\tab\tab preserving the original contents of the specified line].\par
\par
\tab 2)\tab In FOCAL, 1969, \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 L\highlight0  is used to search for the same \tab\tab\tab character as used in previous searches.  In the ED\{it\} \tab\tab\tab command, \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 A\highlight0  is used to search for the same character \tab\tab as used in previous searches.  Note: If no prior searches \tab\tab\tab have been performed, \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 A\highlight0  will output the rest of the \tab\tab\tab line contents [if any] to the system console terminal and \tab\tab\tab wait for futher input.\par
\par
\tab 3)\tab In FOCAL, 1969, \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 J\highlight0  [\highlight3 <LF>\highlight0 ] processes and prints out \tab\tab\tab all remaining characters in the line on the system console \tab\tab\tab terminal; additional editing can be performed ending with \tab\tab\tab\highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 M\highlight0  [\highlight3 <CR>\highlight0 ] to close out the line.  In recent releases \tab\tab of P?S/8, the ED\{it\} command supports \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 F\highlight0  to perform \tab\tab\tab the identical function.\par
\par
\tab 4)\tab Within the ED\{it\} command, \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 J\highlight0  [\highlight3 <LF>\highlight0 ] will output \tab\tab\tab\highlight3 <CR>\highlight0  and \highlight3 <LF>\highlight0  and then the current tentative line contents \tab\tab\tab to the system console terminal.  This is especially useful \tab\tab\tab throughout the keyboard monitor when using hard-copy console \tab\tab terminals which generally require the display of deleted \tab\tab\tab characters [which can be confusing].\par
\par
\tab 5)\tab\highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 U\highlight0  will completely restart the editing operation; \tab\tab\tab this includes the printing of the opening line number and \tab\tab\tab\highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  characters on the system console terminal.  No changes \tab\tab have been made to the specified line.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab 6)\tab When FOCAL, 1969 was designed, the displayed character \tab\tab\tab associated with ASCII code 95 decimal [137 octal] [5F hex] \tab\tab\tab was implemented on early Teletype Model 33 and similar \tab\tab\tab console terminals as \cf2\highlight1\f2\fs29\u8592?\cf0\highlight0\f0\fs24 ; the displayed character was later \tab\tab\tab phased out of the ASCII character set and replaced with \cf2\highlight1\f2 _\cf0\highlight0\f0 .  \tab\tab The FOCAL, 1969 MO\{dify\} command uses this key to delete all \tab\tab text already output to the system console.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab It is apparent this function would have been assigned to an \tab\tab alternate code had the newer displayed character been the \tab\tab\tab usual graphic at the time; most newer software considers \tab\tab\tab this code equivalent to the backspace or delete key as \tab\tab\tab implemented on newer keyboards.\par
\par
\tab\tab Within the ED\{it\} command, \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 K\highlight0  will delete all \tab\tab\tab\tab characters already processed and output to the system \tab\tab\tab\tab console terminal.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: If the ED\{it\} command has not modified the line \tab\tab\tab\tab number, the line number and \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  will be preserved and will \tab\tab output to the system console terminal to continue the \tab\tab\tab  \tab editing process.  If the line number has already been \tab\tab\tab\tab modified or deleted, it will not be restored after\par
\tab\tab\highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 K\highlight0  has deleted all processed line contents.  If \tab\tab\tab\highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 U\highlight0  is used to restart the ED\{it\} command, the line \tab\tab\tab number [and \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0 ] will be restored as described in 5) above.\par
\par
\tab 7)\tab Pressing \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 H\highlight0  [\highlight3 <BS>\highlight0 ] initiates the \i rubout\i0  [delete \tab\tab\tab previous character in the line edit buffer] function.  This \tab\tab can also be accomplished using the DEL [\highlight3 <RO>\highlight0 ] key or \cf2\highlight1\f2 _\cf0\highlight0\f0  \tab\tab\tab [originally displayed as \cf2\highlight1\f2\fs29\u8592?\cf0\highlight0\f0\fs24  on certain older console \tab\tab\tab\tab terminals].\par
\par
\tab\tab The specific implementation of this function is determined \tab\tab\tab by several keyboard monitor settings.  For most hard-copy \tab\tab\tab terminals, each instance echoes the previous character as it \tab\tab is deleted; this basic form of \i rubout\i0  handling can be \tab\tab\tab\tab implemented using the SET RUBOUT ECHO command.  If the SET \tab\tab\tab RUBOUT TOPS10 command is used, all deleted characters will \tab\tab\tab be echoed in reverse order of deletion surrounded by \cf2\highlight1\f2\\\cf0\highlight0\f0  \tab\tab\tab characters.\par
\par
\tab\tab For display terminals, the most widely used method is to \tab\tab\tab output \highlight3 <BS>\highlight0  \highlight3 <SP>\highlight0  \highlight3 <BS>\highlight0  which visually obliterates the deleted \tab\tab character; this can be implemented using the SET RUBOUT \tab\tab\tab BACKSP command.  Additional commands are available for \tab\tab\tab custom support of common DEC terminals such as the VT05, \tab\tab\tab VT52 and VT100.  For more information use the SET /A \tab\tab\tab\tab command for a complete list of terminal customizations and \tab\tab\tab related issues.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
EN\{ter\}\cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  \cf1\i filename:u\cf0\i0\par
\par
\tab A specified [or default from a prior file name command] file name \tab is entered into the directory; no actual file storage is \tab modified.\par
\par
\tab Appendix A contains information on recovering from accidental \tab file deletion using the EN\{ter\} and other keyboard monitor \tab commands along with specific system program utilities [if \tab available].\par
\par
\tab Judicious use of the EN\{ter\} command can place files for maximum \tab efficiency.  On certain tape systems, some programs can access \tab the contents of files faster if the files are arranged in an \tab interleaved manner; the EN\{ter\} command can setup the proper file \tab placement prior to use.\par
\par
\tab For example, on TC01/TC08/TC55/TU56 systems, it is impossible to \tab access adjacent files without causing a tape turnaround.  [Note: \tab This is a hardware restriction.  If a DECtape handler allowed \tab tape motion when the handler exits, the tape would always \tab eventually wind into the forward end zone; the next tape \tab operation would first have to rewind the tape to just below the\par
\tab desired initial tape block, then turn around to cause forward \tab tape motion.\par
\par
\tab Since every call to the system device handler stops the tape upon \tab exit, the next call must be to an area slightly further up the \tab tape yet not immediately adjacent for a forward initial search to \tab be effective.  In the specific case of TFS files, this means \tab attempts to access the immediately adjacent file will cause a \tab tape turnaround; accessing a file an additional file slot [or \tab more] further up the tape will start with forward motion and the \tab tape operation will successfully finish without a tape \tab turnaround.\par
\par
\tab Thus, by arranging the files of a project in an interleaved \tab order, access to the files is greatly increased.  The interleave \tab can be accomplished either by having files from an unrelated \tab project interdigitated with the current project files, or the \tab project files can be split into halves, with the second half \tab files between the first half files; there would be no turnarounds \tab in the former case and a single turnaround in the latter case due \tab to having to rewind to access the second half files.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab The \i slurp\i0  binary loader for TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 always starts \tab with an initial forward search to more quickly access the binary \tab files, which are generally located further up the tape from the \tab loader code image.  The interleaved file approach will decrease \tab file access time for binary files as this further improves the \tab loading process; as such, it would be prudent to interleave \tab binary output files with the source code files of the same \tab project.  Both sets of files will be accessed in an optimal way \tab by the programs generally associated with these files \tab [respectively PAL for source code files and the BIN binary file \tab loader for \i slurp\i0  binary files].  See Note 1.\par
\par
ER\{ase\} [\i line-number-range\i0 ]\par
\par
\tab All lines in the specified \i line-number-range\i0  are removed from the \tab edit buffer.\par
\par
\tab The ER\{ase\} command is often used to split a file into two \tab smaller files when the file is nearly full.  A split-point line \tab number is determined to separate the contents of the current \tab file.\par
\par
\tab\highlight3\f2 Note: Novice users are advised to make a backup copy of the file\highlight0\par
\f0\tab\highlight3\f2 before attempting this to avoid data loss.\highlight0\f0\par
\par
\tab 1)\tab All lines from the split-point onward can be deleted by \tab\tab\tab using the command ER\{ase\} XXXX- [where XXXX is the chosen \tab\tab\tab split-point line number] which creates the smaller file \tab\tab\tab which consists of all lines up to the split-point.  The \tab\tab\tab edit buffer should be WR\{itten\} into the file designated to \tab\tab contain the first part of the former file.\par
\par
\tab 2)\tab The complete [former] file should be reloaded using the \tab\tab\tab FE[tch\} command; all lines from the beginning of the file \tab\tab\tab through the split-point should be deleted using the command \tab\tab ER\{ase\} -YYYY [where YYYY is the value XXXX above less 1] \tab\tab\tab which creates the smaller file which consists of all lines \tab\tab\tab beyond the split-point.  The edit buffer should be WR\{itten\} \tab\tab into the file designated to contain the second part of the \tab\tab\tab former file.\par
\par
\tab Tip: Since the specific line number used in the ranges given to \tab the ER\{ase\} command need not exist [and merely serves as a \tab limit], a line that is exactly one lower than the intended split-\tab point [and non-existent] can be used in both operations when \tab splitting the original file.\par
\par
\tab Tip: The method illustrated is an example of one way to \tab accomplish the file-splitting operation.  By using equivalent \tab alternative steps, the same net result can be achieved by first \tab saving the ending portion of the original file then creating the \tab beginning portion.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab Tip: Seasoned users will reuse the original file name as one of \tab the two descendant files; only one additional file need be \tab specified within the overall stream of project files; the \tab commands used to pass a stream of files to a system program [PAL, \tab FOCAL, etc.] should be updated accordingly.\par
\par
\tab Observation: Many competing systems require full-file editing; \tab this means that sufficient space on the storage device is tied up \tab for roughly twice the size of the entire project at the point of \tab any editing operation; later the obsolete copy is released, \tab generally amounting to an inadequate smaller portion of the \tab storage device that eventually leads to serious fragmentation\par
\tab and overhead issues.  By limiting editing to fixed size portions \tab of the project, it is only necessary to periodically allocate an \tab additional file at a time to resolve line number problems by \tab splitting the file as described above.\par
\par
\tab Note: After repeated editing operations on a source file \tab project, certain files will tend to be nearly full while others \tab may be more sparsely populated.  This can be remedied by \tab periodically using the OS8CON program to create a single file in \tab OS/8 format, then use OS8CON to recreate the source files from \tab the OS/8 format data; OS8CON standardizes the usage of each file \tab thus eliminating problems of variation in file usage.  A report \tab is given of how many files were created; it is conceivable the \tab repacked file set could occupy less files total; however, in rare \tab instances it is conceivable that additional files might be needed \tab due to the standardization.  Additional files can be added to the \tab list of output files as necessary.  The OS8CON program will \tab indicate how many files were actually used; the rest can be \tab discarded.\par
\par
FE\{tch\}\cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  \cf1\i filename:u\cf0\i0\par
\par
\tab A specified [or default from a prior file name command] file name \tab is located within the TFS directory; the file contents are loaded \tab into the edit buffer.  Keyboard monitor/integrated line editor \tab commands may be used to examine or modify the contents of the \tab edit buffer.  The original file is unchanged unless an explicit \tab WR\{ite\} command is performed.\par
\tab\par
\tab Note: Attempts to fetch nonexistent files will cause error \tab messages.\par
\par
HA\{lt\}\par
\par
\tab The computer deliberately stops running.  Press \cf2\highlight1\f2 Continue\cf0\highlight0\f0  [or \tab equivalent if available] to restart the machine; the keyboard \tab monitor continues in the same state it was prior to the machine \tab halting.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab Do not use this command on systems that do not support the \tab\cf2\highlight1\f2 Continue\cf0\highlight0\f0  functionality; the only remedy would be to perform a \tab system restart which would reload the keyboard monitor without \tab saving the edit buffer or other current particulars.\par
\par
\tab Note: It is merely a vicious and unfounded rumor that the \tab command also causes the machine to catch fire.  The PDP-8 \tab supports the HLT instruction to stop program execution; it is the \tab stuff of legend there is the HCF instruction to both Halt and \tab Catch Fire; P?S/8 stops the processor using the HLT instruction\par
\tab which would tend to cool the computer.\cf1\par
\par
\cf0 KI\{ll\}\cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  \cf1\i filename:u\cf0\i0\par
\par
\tab KI\{ll\} [delete] a file from the TFS directory.  The affected file \tab name is removed from the TFS directory; the underlying file \tab contents are not affected, making it possible to restore the file \tab if the deletion was unintended.\par
\par
\tab Note: When a file is deleted from the TFS directory, the free \tab directory slot is available for storage of another file; if this \tab should occur, the original file is lost.\par
\par
\tab Note: The KI\{ll\} command does not check to potentially clear \tab the previous entry after clearing what becomes the first of \tab any final completely empty entries.  This is a deliberate design \tab decision to aid in file recovery; deleted entries generally \tab contain partial file names [unless the file name was only one or \tab two characters].  Appendix A contains information on recovering \tab from accidental file deletion using keyboard monitor commands and \tab specific system program utilities [if available].\cf1\par
\cf0\par
LA\{st\}\par
\par
\tab This command outputs the line number of the highest existing line \tab [if any] to the system console terminal.  All line numbers must \tab be in the range of 0 through 4094 decimal [0 through 7776 octal].\par
\par
\tab Note: Perverse resequencing operations can cause an overflow \tab condition.  If required, the situation can always be rectified \tab without file loss by using smaller line number increments in a \tab subsequent resequence operation [such as merely using the default \tab values].  If the user has some familiarity with the contents of a \tab particular file, the LA\{st\} command can confirm the highest line \tab number is in range; if the line numbers overflow, listing the \tab file contents will show lack of proper line number order.  After \tab a valid resequencing operation, the file will again show ordered \tab line numbers; the LA\{st\} command will output the highest line \tab number as expected.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: In all releases of P?S/8, resequence operations perform an \tab automatic LA\{st\} command; if the user is familiar with the file \tab contents, the effects of line number overflow will be immediately\par
\tab evident.\par
\par
LC\{atalog\}\cf1\i :u\cf0\i0\par
\par
\tab The device logical unit \cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  specified in the LC\{atalog\} command is \tab selected as the new logical unit for all subsequent file-oriented \tab logical unit purposes.  If the \cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  is omitted or the previous \tab logical unit and the new logical unit are identical, the command \tab is effectively ignored.  As required, the corresponding TFS \tab directory on the new logical unit is read into memory replacing \tab the previous one.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: For all operations of the keyboard monitor and integrated \tab editor, the TFS directory on the current logical unit is 100% \tab resident in memory; in many instances, this causes an \tab extraordinary decrease in file access time.\par
\par
\tab The LC\{atalog\} command merely changes the logical unit without o\tab therwise performing another action.\par
\par
\tab Tip: If a series of operations must be performed on a particular \tab logical unit as specified within a BATCH file, the first \tab operation must effect a change to the intended logical unit.  \tab When a BATCH file is created, it is often not possible to \tab determine the intended logical unit until the actual \tab configuration is finalized.  The LC\{atalog\} command can be used \tab to initialize the set of commands placed further into the BATCH \tab file stream [all without local logical unit settings] along with \tab other appropriate initialization such as a series of console \tab messages or PL\{ease\} commands as required.  The LC\{atalog\} \tab command can be located in a separate initialization file to \tab modularize the components of the BATCH operation; as many as 17 \tab control files can be passed at one time to BATCH.\par
\par
LE\{ft\}\par
\par
\tab The free byte [six-bit character] count within the edit buffer is \tab shown on the system console terminal.\par
\par
\tab As more text lines are added to the edit buffer, the free byte \tab count decreases.  A full file condition occurs when there is \tab insufficient free space to insert the latest line contained in \tab the command-line buffer; the \cf2\highlight1\f2 FILE FULL\cf0\highlight0\f0  error message is shown on\tab\tab the system console terminal and the latest command-line contents \tab are lost.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab The LE\{ft\} command can serve as a warning mechanism indicating \tab how much free space is currently available.  If a file is nearly \tab full, corrective remedies can be applied [such as splitting the \tab file into two independent files as detailed in the description of \tab the ER\{ase\} command].\par
\par
\tab All TFS files are 2048 12-bit words [4096 six-bit bytes] long \tab regardless of internal content.  Within TFS text files, two 12-\tab bit words are used for line number and line pointer purposes for \tab each file line.  All lines start on 12-bit word boundaries.  \tab Since each line has its own line pointer, all file modifications \tab are performed nearly instantaneously.\par
\par
  \tab While the line pointers and associated line numbers of all file \tab lines are 12-bit words, the rest of the file predominantly \tab consists of six-bit bytes; lines that end with an odd byte count \tab include a filler byte to maintain 12-bit word alignment.  All \tab file lines are contiguously stored with the line pointer and \tab line number values stored at the back end of the file; there is a \tab single 12-bit word set to zero after the last file line to serve \tab as end of file for all programs that only read text characters.\par
\par
\tab There is a four 12-bit word reserved area; as such, the LE\{ft\} \tab command will report 4088 bytes available in an empty file.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab It is advisable to have a reasonable reserve within every file \tab for future editing purposes [thus avoiding loss of the command \tab line when attempting to add another line to the file].  Most \tab users should consider WR\{iting\} the edit buffer to a file when \tab the available space falls below 200 bytes; the edit buffer can \tab then be cleared with the ER\{ase\} command to start the next file.  \tab For a typical\tab project, the contents of multiple files passed to \tab any system program are concatenated together as a continuous \tab input stream; a list of up to 17 input files may be passed to any \tab system program.\par
\par
LI\{st\} [\i line-number-range\i0 ]\par
\par
\tab All file lines in the edit buffer in the specified \i line-number-\tab range\i0  are output to the system console terminal.\par
\par
\tab Note: Output formatting is affected by the current horizontal tab \tab setting as each line of output starts with the line number \tab followed by \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0 .  This equally applies to additional \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  \tab characters within the file.\par
\par
LN\{umber\} [\i optional-starting-line-number\i0 ] [\i optional-line-increment\i0 ]\par
\par
\tab Auto-sequenced input is initiated starting at the specified\par
\tab\i optional-starting-line-number\i0  [line 100 if none given].  \tab Subsequent lines are incremented by the \i optional-line-increment\i0  \tab value [default is 10 if none given].\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab During text entry, it is possible to delete all or a portion of \tab the sequence number to execute another command [or perhaps change \tab the line number that applies].  Auto-sequence mode can be \tab terminated by pressing \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 D\highlight0 .  The standard P?S/8 text \tab character set applies; lower-case characters will be "folded" to \tab the upper-case equivalent.\par
\par
LT\{ext\} [\i line-number-range\i0 ]\par
\par
\tab The contents of all lines in the specified \i line-number-range\i0  are \tab directed to the system console terminal without the associated \tab line numbers.\par
\par
\tab This is the fastest form of file contents display, which can be \tab helpful on slow system console terminals such as the Teletype \tab Model 33.\par
\par
\tab Note: The command name is derived from the action of listing only \tab the text in the file.\par
\par
\tab Tip: To decrease printing time still further [at the expense of \tab cosmetic formatting], horizontal tab disposition should be set to \tab output a single space character for every \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  character in the \tab file with the TA\{b\} 0 command [or the analogous SET TAB SINGLE \tab utility program option if it is desirable to permanently change \tab the default setting].\par
\par
MO\{nitor\}\par
\par
\tab Invoking the MO\{nitor\} command exits the keyboard monitor; the \tab system is rebooted by restarting the computer at 07600; this is \tab entirely equivalent to a manual restart at the same address.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: Using the MO\{nitor\} command in BATCH files may change the \tab overall flow of BATCH operations depending on specific BATCH \tab options chosen.  A BATCH file under development may include the \tab MO\{nitor\} command to temporarily limit the overall scope of the \tab BATCH file as necessary.  Additional information about the P?S/8 \tab BATCH utility is available in other documents.\par
\par
PL\{ease\} [character string]\par
\par
\tab Executing the PL\{ease\} command will display the entire command \tab line [including any character string past the command] on the \tab system console terminal.  All operations are paused until any \tab non-control character is pressed [which will otherwise be \tab ignored].\par
\par
\tab A typical usage of the PL\{ease\} command is within a BATCH file to \tab allow some external action to be applied:\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab .^^ GET PROGRAM LISTING BATCH CONTROL FILE.\par
\tab .^^\par
\tab .^^ LAST EDIT: 07-APR-2015 CJL\par
\tab .^^\par
\tab .PLEASE TURN ON THE LINE PRINTER OR PRESS CONTROL-C TO ABORT ...\par
\cf1\tab [A character is pressed by the operator which is not echoed.]\cf0\par
\tab .^^\par
\tab .^^ THANK YOU FOR ENABLING THE LINE PRINTER; CONTINUING ...\par
\par
\tab Certain P?S/8 programs output to the system line printer [if \tab available] otherwise falling back on the system console terminal.\tab [Some programs have command-line options to prevent discovery of \tab the line printer which is the default action.]  By inserting \tab appropriate lines into the BATCH file similar to the above \tab example, the BATCH can be run as intended.\par
\par
\tab Note: All of the active control characters are monitored for \tab their normal function during the command pause initiated by the\tab\tab PL\{ease\} command; the system remains paused until another non-\tab control character is pressed.\par
\par
\tab Tip: The PL\{ease\} command can cause the system to be paused \tab while character echo is off.  When designing BATCH scripts, it is \tab recommended the control file output appropriate messages before\par
 \tab and after executing the PL\{ease\} command to prevent confusion.\par
\par
R \i system-program\cf1 :u\cf0\i0  [options]\par
\par
\tab Load the specified \i system-program\i0  initial segment into memory and \tab start at the starting address indicated in the system  programs \tab directory.  Various optional parameters are allowed:\par
\par
\tab 1)\tab Files.\par
\par
\tab\tab An optional list of output files or input files [or both] \tab\tab\tab can be passed to a system program.  Output files [if any] \tab\tab\tab must precede input files [if any].  The maximum number of \tab\tab\tab files is 17.  Output files are indicated by \cf2\highlight1\f2 <\cf0\highlight0\f0  after the last \tab\tab output file specification; input files [if any] follow \tab\tab\tab output files [if present].\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: Specific system programs may further restrict the \tab\tab\tab maximum passed file count.\par
\par
\tab 2)\tab =\i xxxx\i0  parameter.\par
\par
\tab\tab This is a numerical value generally expressed in octal in \tab\tab\tab the range of 0 through 7776 [0 through 4094 decimal].  While \tab\tab 7777 [4095 decimal] cannot expressed directly, this value is \tab\tab used by default when the =\i xxxx\i0  is not used.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab Note: Certain system programs allow the use of the current \tab\tab\tab radix in effect for the =\i xxxx\i0  parameter; most programs force \tab\tab octal radix.\par
\par
\tab 3)\tab Option switches.\par
\par
\tab\tab The option switches include \cf2\highlight1\f2 /A\cf0\highlight0\f0  through \cf2\highlight1\f2 /Z\cf0\highlight0\f0  and \cf2\highlight1\f2 /0\cf0\highlight0\f0  through \cf2\highlight1\f2 /9\cf0\highlight0\f0 .  \tab\tab When expressing switches in groups, the individual \cf2\highlight1\f2 /\cf0\highlight0\f0  is \tab\tab\tab replaced with a leading \cf2\highlight1\f2 (\cf0\highlight0\f0  followed by one or more option \tab\tab\tab switches, each expressed as a single character, followed by \tab\tab an optional trailing \cf2\highlight1\f2 )\cf0\highlight0\f0 .\par
\par
\tab All options can be included at any point after the system program \tab name; all values [other than file name lists] can be specified \tab redundantly without error.  \highlight3 <SP>\highlight0  and \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  characters are freely \tab allowed between values to clarify the command formation if \tab desired.\par
\par
\tab To maintain backward compatibility with the R-L Monitor System,\par
\tab\cf2\highlight1\f2 ,\cf0\highlight0\f0  is allowed as an alternate file name separator [with or without \tab additional \highlight3 <SP>\highlight0  or \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  characters].  Input file lists may be \tab preceded with \cf2\highlight1\f2 <\cf0\highlight0\f0  even if no output files are specified.\par
\par
\tab The position-optimized files % and $ are freely allowed as input \tab [or output] files.  New output files will be automatically \tab entered into the TFS directory.\par
\par
\tab Choice of input and output file specification.\par
\par
\tab As of the release of P?S/8 Version 8Y, the R command can also be \tab expressed with the lists of files reversed.  Input files can be \tab specified first followed by \cf2\highlight1\f2 >\cf0\highlight0\f0  then the output files.  Command \tab formation is now more flexible and reminiscent of certain newer \tab operating systems.\par
\par
\tab Seasoned users tend to prefer the original implementation; newer \tab users will likely prefer specifying input files first since many \tab modern operating systems form analogous commands in this manner.\par
\par
\tab Note: By having both methods of file specification available,\par
 \tab P?S/8 takes a neutral stand on this design issue; however, \tab regardless of command form, output files are always processed \tab before input files as discussed below.\par
\par
\tab Logical unit considerations.\par
\par
\tab The default logical unit for file names is the current logical \tab unit in effect.  This may have been changed from the bootup \tab logical unit by commands issued prior to the R command.\par
\par
\tab The default logical unit for the \i system-program\i0  is the bootup \tab logical unit.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab Both \i system-program\i0  and file specifications can override the \tab prevailing default logical units using explicit \cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  constructs as \tab is the case in various other keyboard monitor commands.  Explicit \tab logical unit changes in file specifications update the default \tab value for any additional file specifications on the command line.\par
\par
\tab By design, all output files are processed before all input files.\par
\par
\tab Concise program execution.\par
\par
\tab When the first two characters of the \i system-program\i0  name are not \tab in conflict with keyboard monitor commands, the \i system-program\i0  \tab may be invoked directly [including all options].  Depending on \tab the P?S/8 system release, this may cause the command process to \tab commence sooner [due to lower internal overhead]; in certain \tab instances, concise program execution may be the only constructive \tab way to use the program due to dependence on the availability of \tab the edit buffer [which is not preserved when the \i system-program\i0  \tab is invoked by the R command].\par
\par
\tab Extended-length File Issues.\par
\par
\tab Most P?S/8 system programs are capable of reading the textual \tab contents of extended-length files created by conversion utilities \tab designed for the specific intersystem process addressed.  The \tab OS8CON conversion utility can access data on a logical drive unit \tab of the P?S/8 system device handler if certain compatibility \tab requirements are met [which is often the case].  Other utilities \tab such as L6DCON require internal device handlers due to the \tab inherent incompatibility of the underlying media [256 words/block \tab LINCtapes on either the LINC-8 or the PDP-12].  Appendix D \tab described a tentative program to transmit paper-tape text data \tab to/from P?S/8 including the optional use of extended-length \tab files.  In all such programs, extended-length output files can be \tab created specifying particular command-line option switches. \tab After conversion or other processing is completd, extended-length \tab files can be passed as input files to most system programs [such \tab as PAL, DUMP and FOCAL] that ignore file line numbers.\par
\par
\tab All of these programs properly handle extended-length output \tab files using the definition of the extended directory associated \tab with P?S/8 Version 8Y.  Future releases will streamline the \tab implementation to conform with new structures associated with the \tab release of P?S/8 Version 9A; there will be minimal impact on the \tab user once the new structures are fully supported.  See Note 2.\par
\par
\tab Note: There had been plans to allow  full support of multiple \tab extended-length files per device; as such, there were partially-\tab implemented structures added as a framework for this effort.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab This line of system development has been abandoned in favor of \tab the P?S/8 SHELL overlay, which will provide more comprehensive \tab directory support using an entirely different user interface \tab model.  \par
\par
\tab Future P?S/8 releases will support logical device units with a \tab single extended-length file which will be compatible with all \tab basic P?S/8 system programs that ignore file line numbers [such \tab as BATCH, BLKCPY, DUMP, FOCAL, PAL and all prevailing filesystem \tab conversion programs].  The SET utility will be upgraded to \tab provide enhanced support of extended-length files including user \tab file name preference as well as enhanced raw support for use with \tab programs such as the PAL symbolic cross-reference facility.\par
\par
RA\{dix\} [8 or 10]\par
\par
\tab When the RA\{dix\} command is invoked without an optional \tab parameter, the radix for all numerical input or output is \tab reversed to the opposite of the current value in effect.  This \tab applies to all contexts where a numerical value may be expressed \tab including file line numbers, passing parameters to programs using \tab the =\i xxxx\i0  construct [under certain circumstances] and the \tab numerical output of commands such as LA\{st\} and LE\{ft\}.\par
\par
\tab The radix can be set by passing an explicit parameter: RA\{dix\} 10 \tab will set the radix to decimal; RA\{dix\} 8 will set the radix to \tab octal.\par
\par
\tab Note: The current radix does not affect the interpretation of the \tab passed parameter; the current radix is saved and then set \tab internally to decimal.  If the command fails due to improper \tab input, the previous radix is restored.\par
\par
\tab Changes made with the RA[dix] command revert to the system \tab default value upon system restart; the scope of the RA\{dix\} \tab command is within the current keyboard monitor session only.  \tab While the usual overall default is decimal, this may be changed \tab using the SET RADIX EIGHT command.\par
\par
\tab The radix chosen for most work is a user preference setting.  \tab Certain limited examples of octal output may be useful to explain \tab basic PDP-8 assembly concepts as illustrated below.\par
\par
\tab .RADIX 8\par
\pard\tx1260\tx2520\tx3780\tx4860\tx6120\par
     .SEQUENCE 177 1\par
\par
      177            *200                    /GET THE DESIRED ORIGIN.\par
      200    BEGIN,  TAD     (7)             /GET A <BEL> CHARACTER.\par
      201            TLS                     /RING IT.\par
      202            JMP I   (7600)          /RESTART THE SYSTEM NOW.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\pard\tab Note: The above example is not an actual assembly but it does \tab cosmetically resemble a simplified assembly listing.  Beginners \tab can learn certain practical aspects of assembly language before \tab mastering additional concepts needed to carry out assembly \tab language programming on a more complete basis.  Since the line \tab numbers are permanent [assuming the edit buffer is WR\{itten\} to a \tab file], small changes can be made which will reflect the way the \tab program actually loads into memory.\par
\par
RE\{sequence\} [\i optional-starting-line-number\i0 ] [\i optional-line-increment\i0 ]\par
\par
\tab The RE[sequence] command reorders the line numbers within the \tab edit buffer as specified by the \i optional-starting-line-number\i0  and\par
\i\tab optional-line-increment\i0  accordingly.  After the resequencing \tab operation [which is carried out virtually instantly], all file \tab lines will be in equal increment order.  Note: No changes are \tab made to the text contents of nor the relative order of the lines; \tab however, all future references to the lines must be made with the \tab new line number values.\par
\par
\tab Resequencing is required when a section of a file has been \tab heavily modified adding in lines with numeric values in between \tab original lines.  This can leave little room for additional lines \tab within the section.  By setting relatively large line increments, \tab it is possible to set aside sufficient numerical space in the \tab file for the purpose of adding lines [typically with an increment \tab of 1]. Depending on the scope of the editing operation, it may be \tab necessary to resequence the file more than once.\par
\par
\tab Tip: Assume a file is sequenced with an increment of 10.  There \tab is the possibility of adding in lines 101 through 109 between \tab line 100 and line 110.  The ED[it] command can be used to create \tab a line 129 from the contents of line 120 [which can then be \tab deleted].  By changing one line, there is now the possibility of \tab adding in lines 101 through 128.  If the file is resequenced [or \tab was created with even larger increments] then more than enough \tab line number space can be available for any reasonable edit \tab session.\par
\par
\tab Note: In certain editing situations, the limiting factor may be \tab file capacity; it may be necessary to first split the file into \tab two independent files before applying localized changes.  For \tab more information regarding file splitting, see the description of \tab the ER\{ase\} command.\par
\par
\tab Once a file is stable, it is always recommended to perform a \tab final resequence operation to the default values.\par
\par
\tab After the resequencing, an automatic LA[st] command is performed.  \tab All line numbers must be in the range of 0 through 4094 [0 \tab through 7776 in octal radix].\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab Note: It is possible to overflow the line number range by either \tab using a starting line number that is too high or a line increment \tab that is too large; this can always be remedied by using smaller \tab parameters such as the default values of starting at line 100 \tab with a line increment of 10.\par
\par
RU\{n\} \i system-program\cf1 :u\cf0\i0  [options]\par
\par
\tab The RU\{n\} command is equivalent to the R command; all\par
\tab parameter considerations are identical in both commands.  See \tab Note 2.\par
\par
TA\{b\} [0 3 5]\par
\par
\tab The printing disposition of the \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  character within the \tab keyboard monitor can be customized in various ways to achieve the \tab usual DEC-style alignment to the nearest eight spaces.  All \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  \tab characters are affected such as the printed separation between \tab the line number and the file line text, or any <HT> characters \tab within the text.\par
\par
\tab The \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  character can be displayed in one of four operating \tab modes:\par
\par
\tab 1) \cf2\highlight1\f2 TA\cf0\highlight0\f0\tab The \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  is printed as an actual \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  character; the \tab\tab\tab\tab system console terminal must support DEC-style tab \tab\tab\tab\tab stops every eight columns.  This is the most efficient \tab\tab\tab operating mode assuming there is hardware support for \tab\tab\tab\tab the \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  character; this is generally true on most \tab\tab\tab\tab modern terminals or terminal emulators.\par
\par
\tab 2) \cf2\highlight1\f2 TA 5\cf0\highlight0\f0\tab This mode is similar to 1) above except \highlight3 <NUL>\highlight0  \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab characters are used as filler characters as required \tab\tab\tab\tab for custom support of the Teletype Model 35.  The exact \tab\tab\tab number of filler characters required is dependent on \tab\tab\tab\tab the relative position of the carriage within the latest \tab\tab\tab eight-position tab stop.  For certain positions, two \tab\tab\tab\tab filler characters are required; for some other \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab positions one filler is sufficient.  For two specific\par
\tab\tab\tab positions, no filler is required.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab Note: This is the proper method to handle this terminal \tab\tab\tab device; in certain software systems, the Teletype Model \tab\tab\tab 35 is incorrectly handled by always using a single \tab\tab\tab\tab filler character.  This is a needless print delay in \tab\tab\tab\tab some instances and inadequate wait time in other \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab situations which will cause printhead ink smearing.\page\cf1 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] commands [continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab 3) \cf2\highlight1\f2 TA 3\cf0\highlight0\f0\tab This mode simulates horizontal tab stops by outputting \tab\tab\tab the proper number of \highlight3 <SP>\highlight0  characters to maintain \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab alignment; this is required on printing terminals such \tab\tab\tab as the Teletype Model 33 with no actual horizontal tab \tab\tab\tab support.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab This is the slowest operating mode with the single \tab\tab\tab\tab advantage of universally functioning on all console \tab\tab\tab\tab devices.\par
\par
\tab 4) \cf2\highlight1\f2 TA 0\cf0\highlight0\f0\tab The \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  character is printed as one space character; \tab\tab\tab\tab this is an option for very slow hard-copy terminals \tab\tab\tab\tab where the trade-off of print time versus cosmetics may \tab\tab\tab be a factor.\par
\par
\tab The TA\{b\} command allows temporary override of the permanent \tab setting for \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  printing.  Users of slow terminals may opt to \tab change the operating mode from \cf2\highlight1\f2 TA 3\cf0\highlight0\f0  to \cf2\highlight1\f2 TA 0\cf0\highlight0\f0  to more quickly \tab obtain a minimal listing.\par
\par
\tab The system default setting is TA\{b\} 3 for maximum compatibility \tab with most terminals.  This can be modified using an appropriate \tab sub-option of the system SET utility TAB option.\par
\par
WR\{ite\}\cf1\i :u\cf0\i0  \cf1\i filename:u\cf0\i0\par
\par
\tab The edit buffer contents will be saved in the stated [or implied \tab from a prior file name usage] file slot.  The TFS directory will \tab be updated first to allocate a new file slot if necessary; if the \tab slot exists, the file will be written out directly.\par
\par
\tab Note: On systems based on DECtape or other serial access \tab devices, the time savings when updating a file can be profound.\par
\par
^^ [optional comment string]\par
\par
\tab ^^ is a non-action command to the keyboard monitor [and is \tab equivalent to the REM statement as used in most BASIC \tab implementations or C\{omment\} as implemented in FOCAL, 1969].  ^^ \tab can be used alone or with an optional trailing string of \tab additional characters which are ignored.  This is often used as \tab internal documentation within BATCH files.\par
\par
\tab Tip: The output of BATCH files can be controlled by judicious \tab use of the EC\{ho\} command.  As such, ^^ statements can be used \tab to document internal operations of the BATCH file [with no \tab intention of display during normal operation of the program], or \tab may be deliberately sent to the system console printer as part of \tab the overall BATCH operation.  ^^ can also be used in conjunction \tab with PL\{ease\} commands to acknowledge the operator's response.\page\f2\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\cf3\f0 P?S/8 1989 [Version 8Z] Footnotes.\cf0\par
\par
 1)\tab Binary loading of P?S/8 \i slurp\i0  binary files is accomplished using \tab the P?S/8 BIN system program utility.  This program actually has \tab several operating modes initiated by various command-line option \tab switches which enable one of several paper-tape utility modes; \tab the default mode is to load the \i slurp\i0  loader which is the only \tab exception to the normal permanent residency of the system device \tab handler [other than when loading the binary paper-tape \b BIN\b0  and \tab\b RIM\b0  loader programs].  The system can still be restarted in the \tab normal manner because a device-specific bootstrap is placed at \tab 07600 when the \i slurp\i0  loader is moved over the system device \tab handler.  Various loading options are available to enhance the \tab loading process by the use of specific command-line option \tab switches, some of which have equivalent meaning when used in \tab certain of the paper-tape utility modes.  Additional information \tab about other functions and options of the BIN program is available \tab in other documents.\par
