 2)\tab In an ongoing development effort to lower the internal overhead \tab of the R and RU\{n\} operation which was started in earlier \tab releases of P?S/8, future releases will handle extended-length \tab files in a manner consistent with the planned simplified \tab directory structure.  Overall command execution and keyboard \tab monitor swaps [where relevant] will occur faster; in certain \tab instances, system programs that act as overlays to the keyboard \tab monitor will have increased access to resident routines.  There \tab will be no impact on the user interface once these changes are \tab implemented.\page\f2\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f0\par
Appendix A.\par
\par
Recovery from accidental deletion of TFS files.\par
\par
As is the case in any file system, it is possible to delete TFS files as the result of unintended acts.  Various stategies will be offered to maximize the possibility of proper file recovery.\par
\par
1)\tab Recovering a file accidentally deleted while the file is in the \tab edit buffer.\par
\par
\tab If the file is still available in the edit buffer, it can be \tab saved to the TFS directory by executing the appropriate file save \tab operation [the SA\{ve\} command in older releases, the WR\{ite\} \tab command in newer releases].\par
\par
\tab For most users of P?S/8, this is the only remedial action needed.  \tab However, if file placement is an issue, it may be necessary to \tab place dummy file entries to occupy empty file slots before the \tab intended storage blocks.  The CA[talog] command can be used to \tab confirm the existence of empty entries prior to the desired \tab storage blocks.  The EN[ter] command can be used as many times as \tab necessary to achieve the desired results.  [Note: Precise file \tab placement can be deferred as long as the file is properly saved \tab somewhere within the TFS directory structure.]\par
\par
2)\tab Recovering a file accidentally deleted and the only known copy is \tab stored in a TFS directory slot.\par
\par
\tab No file recovery is possible if the file slot is overwritten; as \tab long as the file slot is available with the original file \tab contents, recovery is still possible.\par
\par
\tab If the proper file slot is evident, an EN\{ter\} command can be \tab issed to recover the file contents and original file name.  The \tab file must be brought into the edit buffer for verification of the \tab expected contents.\par
\par
\tab Note:  The EN\{ter\} command does not check the contents of the \tab corresponding file storage blocks.  Under perverse circumstances, \tab the system may produce junk output from the LI\{st\} or related \tab commands, or possibly hang the keyboard monitor program when the \tab contents of the corresponding blocks are loaded based on \tab presumption of file validity that turn out to be wrong.  \tab Unitialized tape blocks \tab do not represent a proper TFS text file.  \tab By design, no portion of the system device will be accidentally \tab written in such a case; \tab it will be necessary to restart the \tab system at 07600 or other alternate startup methods.\page\cf1 Appendix A [Continued].\cf3\f2\par
\cf0\f0\par
\tab As of this writing, there are no utilities to reveal the name of \tab partially deleted files; future releases of the P?S/8 DIRECT \tab utility will add in the ability to view the file name remnant.  \tab [Note: Deleted TFS files do not retain the first two file name \tab characters; any additional characters in the file name can be \tab viewed with a proper utility.  In the near-term, advanced users \tab of the P?S/8 DUMP program can carry out file name repairs using \tab the ZAP function.]\par
\par
\tab If there is sufficient confusion regarding the proper file slot \tab for recovery, the recommended procedure is to fill all available \tab file slots with dummy names using the EN\{ter\} command as many \tab times as needed.  If available, the P?S/8 DIRECT utility can \tab identify the file type of each file by the use of appropriate \tab command-line options.  [Note: This operation can take significant \tab time to execute; every file is loaded and checked for internal \tab signatures.]\par
\par
\tab If desired, all slots in the TFS directory can be filled with \tab dummy names prior to use.  This will allow any relative file \tab placement for optimization purposes such as the method detailed \tab in the description of the EN\{ter] command.\par
\par
\tab With every available file slot filled, file recovery will \tab generally become a trivial task; file deletion will readily \tab reveal empty file slots.\par
\par
\tab Future releases of the FILMAN utility may support aliased entry \tab points for common file operations such as MOVE and RENAME.  An \tab UNDELE\{TE\} alias can be added that could guide the user through \tab file recovery limiting choices to partially matching deleted file \tab names only.  [Note: It will be a challenge to implement certain \tab of these features unless the operational name is changed or \tab existing keyboard monitor commands are renamed; however, the \tab internal commands of FILMAN can be expanded to support such \tab operations within the context of the FILMAN utility itself.]\par
\par
End of Appendix A.\page\f2\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f0\par
Appendix B.\par
\par
P?S/8 keyboard monitor command names and related design considerations.\par
\par
The P?S/8 keyboard monitor and integrated editor commands are stored internally in block 0013 of the system device as a table of two word entries:\par
\par
1)\tab A 12-bit word containing the six-bit code for the command \tab [inverted],\par
\par
2)\tab The 12-bit address of the command within the keyboard monitor and \tab integrated editor as loaded into memory.\par
\par
The entries repeat until an internal terminator past the last command entry.  It is possible to modify the table to prevent certain commands if desired; all standard systems allow all commands per se.  [Note: The ED\{it\} command is inhibited during BATCH operations only.]  There is no defined order to the implemented commands; for the most part, they are implemented in alphabetical order.\par
\par
While not necessarily ever implemented, various command name changes have been considered for the P?S/8 keyboard monitor and integrated line editor.  Some of these ideas and related issues follow:\par
\par
 1)\tab The PA\{use\} command was first used in the R\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 L Monitor System.  \tab Since this is in conflict with [at the very least] the PAL \tab program for concise program execution, the command was changed to \tab PL\{ease\} first in DIBOL-8, COS\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 300/310 and then P?S/8 some time \tab after the earliest release of COS-300.  As a result, practical \tab usage of the command is to embellish it to PLEASE which \tab represents a slight difference in the scope of the usage of the \tab command to alert the operator to some issue that must be resolved \tab immediately.  The ^^ command [which does nothing but output the \tab entire command line unless echo is turned off] is often used as a \tab follow-up command:\par
\par
    .PLEASE ENABLE THE LINE PRINTER FOR SUBSEQUENT ASSEMBLIES.  [The \tab operator presses any key on the system console terminal keyboard \tab and system operation continues, generally in BATCH mode.]\par
\par
    .^^ THANK YOU\par
\par
 2)\tab The SE\{quence\} command was first used in the R-L Monitor System; \tab this usage remained until the 1976 releases of P?S/8.  Since this \tab is in conflict with utility programs such as SET [which in the \tab 1976 release of P?S/8 is known as VARY], the command was changed \tab in DIBOL-8 and COS-300/310 to LN\{umber\}.\page\cf1 Appendix B [Continued].\cf3\f2\par
\cf0\f0\par
\tab P?S/8 was released in several stages of transition.  The 1976 \tab release of P?S/8 still supports the SE\{quence\} command; however a \tab short-lived NU\{mber\} command was also present with the same \tab functionality.  Newer releases of P?S/8 deleted the SE\{quence\} \tab and NU\{mber\} commands in favor of the LN\{umber\} command.  [Note: \tab It is conceivable that tentative information about the command \tab change in DIBOL-8 was subsequently corrected to LN[umber] \tab however, the information was unofficially communicated to the \tab designers of P?S/8 as NU\{mber\}.  As such, the original SE\{quence\} \tab command was not removed at that time until better documentation \tab was available which shows the actual command is LN\{umber\}.  When \tab DIBOL-8 was first released, it was difficult to obtain \tab documentation; the product was rushed to market.\par
\par
\tab Note: It is well-known fact that DEC management was unaware that \tab the DIBOL-8 system was actually a hacked-up version of the R-L \tab Monitor System, a product they had already rejected when offered \tab to them gratis; they simply could not comprehend that this was a \tab complete operating system at the time far better than most of \tab their other software.  In future years, P?S/8 was also offered to \tab DEC management for the "price" of permanent equipment donation to \tab the Brooklyn Polytechnic Institute [now a division of NYU] [which \tab DEC could have easily converted into a valuable writeoff perhaps \tab leading to a net profit; certain equipment was \b loaned \b0 to the \tab school; however, negotiations broke off with regard to making the \tab computer at the heart of the matter [an 8K PDP-12] become \tab permanently the property of the school [despite the fact that \tab this system was near the end of its economic lifetime and would \tab have to be sold as used equipment].  P?S/8 was shown to DEC \tab personnel twice; each time, indifferent managers rejected it \tab despite massive approval by the members of the PDP-8 programming \tab staff.\par
\par
 3)\tab In the interest of future system programs that might be named \tab RESOUR\{CE\} or similar, the RE\{sequence\} command could be renamed \tab to the LR\{esequence\} command which would provide a sense of \tab symmetry with the LN\{umber\} command.\par
\par
 4)\tab The R-L Monitor System uses the MO\{nitor\} command to restart the \tab system at location 07600 [which is the PDP-8 standard address for \tab rebooting any operating system].  Any of a number of conforming \tab operating systems can be mounted just before entering the command \tab to reboot to another system.\f2\fs16\par
\f0\fs24\par
\tab Note: The R-L Monitor System only runs on TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 \tab DECtape hardware; when the R-L Monitor System was implemented, \tab the other relevant operating systems for this configuration were \tab the DECtape Library System for TC01/TC08 and POLY BASIC [which \tab was developed on the R-L Monitor System].  Due to its inept \tab design, the Disk Monitor System on DECtape cannot be booted via \tab this method; a somewhat longer bootstrap program is required \tab which is incompatible with the R-L Monitor's system kernel.\page\cf1 Appendix B [Continued].\cf0\f2\fs16\par
\f0\fs24\par
\tab OS/8 and COS-300/310 were released several years after the R-L \tab Monitor System [and early experimental versions of P?S/8]; these \tab systems are also compatible with this restart convention with the \tab additional requirement of extended memory [at least 8K memory is \tab needed except in certain configurations that require 12K memory].  \tab In the earliest stages of development, the forerunner to OS/8 \tab [and PS/8] was booted using the R-L Monitor System's MO\{nitor\} \tab command; the R-L Monitor System was used to make patches before \tab attempting additional test cycles.\par
\par
\tab When POLY BASIC [later known as EDU-SYSTEM-30 and sold by DEC] \tab was \tab developed, the notion of leaving BASIC for an underlying [or \tab alternate] operating system was provided by the SY\{stem\} command.  \tab By the use of the SY\{stem\} command, any of these operating \tab systems for compatible hardware can be booted.  Thus, an \tab alternate notion for the command to initiate the restart \tab operation was considered for P?S/8 as the SY\{stem\} command to \tab replace the MO\{nitor\} command which would also simplify migrating \tab between systems.\par
\par
 \tab A related issue is the P?S/8 ED[it] command which is patterned \tab after the FOCAL, 1969 MO\{dify\} command.  If the MO\{nitor\} command \tab was removed, the editing function could be called MO\{dify\} for \tab consistency with FOCAL, 1969.  None of these changes were made in \tab 1976 and the matter was deferred in favor of an unrelated issue.  \tab [See 5) below.]\par
\par
 5)  When the concept of logical units was added to P?S/8, it became \tab clear the syntax to implement this could be added to many \tab commands that would be appropriate.  For example, the CA\{talog\} \tab command could implement a trailing \cf3\i :u\cf0\i0  where \cf3\i u\cf0\i0  was a logical unit \tab in the range of 0 through 7.  However, this would then require \tab performing an explicit operation; merely changing the logical \tab unit without otherwise performing some operation was not \tab available.\par
\par
     As such, the first implementation of a command to change the \tab logical device unit [and perform no other function] was \tab implemented in early P?S/8 releases as SY\{stem\}\cf3\i :u\cf0\i0 .  Since the \cf3\i :u\cf0\i0  \tab syntax is optional, the command SY\{stem\} without a logical unit \tab specification merely reloads the current TFS directory [or \tab catalog as it often referred to].\par
\par
     It became clear that the SY\{stem\} command would be in conflict \tab with future programs such as the SYSTAT\{us\} utility program that \tab provides various system configuration values.  With the release \tab of P?S/8 Version 8Y, the command to merely load the TFS directory \tab from a new logical unit was changed to the LC\{atalog\}\cf3\i :u\cf0\i0  command \tab which allowed the ability for programs that start with SY to be \tab developed.\page\cf1 Appendix B [Continued].\cf0\f2\fs16\par
\f0\fs24\par
\tab This interim release also included a transitional version of the \tab SYSTAT\{us\} utility program.  [Note: As of the release of P?S/8 \tab Version 8Z, SYSTAT\{us\} is mostly functional; however, it includes \tab primitive support for various internal system functions that are \tab subject to change or complete removal in light of the P?S/8 SHELL \tab overlay currently under development.]\par
\par
     Thus, there will be no additional consideration of a command to \tab reload the operating system to be named SY\{stem\}.  As of P?S/8 \tab Version 8Z the MO\{nitor\} command is used to reload the operating \tab system.  The intra-line editing function of the integrated line \tab editor is presently invoked with the ED\{it\} command and not \tab likely to be changed in the future.  [Note: A SYSTEM program \tab utility could be written perhaps to be a more general bootstrap \tab program to support startup from multiple devices; however, it is \tab more likely such a program would be named BOOT with various \tab command-line options as required.]\par
\par
 6)  Historically, the overall size of computer memory was referred to \tab as the \i core size \i0 ; the earliest memory implementations as used in \tab most PDP-8 models were measured my this method [such as 4K core].  \tab As such, the P?S/8 program to control the logical size of memory \tab is known as the CORE utility.\par
\par
     It is feasible to change the program name to MEMORY if necessary; \tab however, the original program name applies as of P?S/8 Version \tab 8Z.\par
\par
     If the keyboard monitor is ever modified to include an additional \tab command, it is conceivable it could be known as the CO\{py\} \tab command.  If this is ever implemented, the name of the memory \tab size management utility could be changed to MEMORY or other non-\tab conflicting name.\par
\par
\tab If additional programs are written to add this functionality\tab [without adding a command to the keyboard monitor] any of these \tab methods would be feasible.  A COPY program utility would have its \tab own command syntax, presumably to implement a variety of file \tab copy functions.  [Note:  P?S/8 Version 8Z includes the FILMAN \tab utility to perform several file-related functions including \tab selectable copy and delete.  If desired, the notion of a COPY \tab program could be aliased to the FILMAN program by implementing \tab appropriate command-line switches to force the operating mode.\page\cf1 Appendix B [Continued].\cf0\par
\par
\tab [Note: P?S/8 already supports several aliased programs.  The \tab START program is actually an alias within the BIN utility \tab program; the GET program is actually another alias within the BIN \tab utility program.  The BIN utility program loads P?S/8 \i slurp\i0  \tab binary files or performs various paper-tape-oriented utility \tab functions [including loading in a version of the stand-alone DEC \tab binary paper-tape \b BIN\b0  and \b RIM\b0  loaders] depending on various \tab command-line options.  The START utility program starts up the \tab latest saved memory image and shares certain command-line options \tab with the BIN program.  The GET utility program is equivalent to \tab invoking the BIN program with certain options forced for the \tab purpose of loading and saving an image of memory after the \tab loading is completed.  The three commands are inter-related; all \tab are actually different entries into the same system program.]\par
\par
 7)  In early releases of P?S/8, the command to remove a selected \tab range of lines was implemented as the DE\{lete\} [\i line-number-\tab range\i0 ] command, which is consistent with the R-L Monitor System.  \tab This was changed in Version 8Y to ER\{ase\} [\i line-number-range\i0 ] for \tab greater compatibility with DIBOL and COS-300/310.  As discussed \tab in 6) above, an additional entry could be added to the FILMAN \tab utility to add a DELETE program function to take advantage of the \tab various options available in the FILMAN utility [such as wild-\tab card matches or file filters].  This functionality is already \tab available in FILMAN, but a DELETE program entry would allow an \tab alternate way to approach command file management functions.\par
\par
 8)  The complexity of the FILMAN program should allow a file move \tab capability.  As such, the issues surrounding the MO\{nitor\} \tab command may be reconsidered; there would be the need to implement \tab a MOVE alias entry to the FILMAN program; this would necessitate \tab changing the system restart function to a different command.  \tab Tentative replacements could include the EX\{it\} [or EX\{odus\}] \tab command or perhaps the BY\{e\} command.\par
\par
 9)  Due to the trade-offs of supporting high-speed paper-tape devices \tab versus the ability to support more important features, the former \tab HR\{eader\} and PU\{nch\} commands were removed from P?S/8 in the \tab Version 8U release.  The functions needed are best served in a \tab utility program outside of the scope of the keyboard monitor and \tab integrated line editor.  As of this writing, the \i PTUTIL\i0  program \tab is being implemented for release as an add-on to P?S/8 Version \tab 8Z.  For more information, consult the \ul\i PTUTIL Technical Guide\ulnone\i0  as \tab it becomes available.\page\cf1 Appendix B [Continued].\cf3\f2\par
\cf0\f0\par
10)\tab P?S/8 supports a wide variety of console terminals, some of which \tab have limited throughput when compared to higher-speed devices.  \tab As such, the needs of users of Teletypes and related devices must \tab be served carefully; always outputting the best format \tab cosmetically speaking severely shortchanges users who are merely \tab interested in a rough listing of the contents of a file in a no-\tab frills manner in the shortest amount of time.  As such, a \tab stripped-down version of the original PU[nch] command was \tab retained in later releases known as the LT\{ext\} command.  The \tab line number and \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  character associated with each file line are \tab omitted; only the text characters of the line are sent to the \tab system console terminal.  Using the TA\{b\} 0 command, an unadorned \tab raw version of the text can be obtained in the shortest possible \tab time.\par
\par
     While there are no plans to change this command, another command \tab name considered was the LC\{ontents\} command to emphasise that \tab only the contents of the lines are printed, not the line numbers.  \tab As is always the case, the design goal is to provide an intuitive \tab command name that is also not in conflict with program names that \tab can be as long as six characters.  With DIBOL-8/COS-300/310\par
     compatibility already achieved, other command name changes are \tab always possible to deal with design elements unique to the P?S/8 \tab environment.  [Note: The P?S/8 SHELL will not have an integrated \tab editor; the design issues are markedly different.]\par
\par
11)  With the introduction of the FILMAN utility in P?S/8 Version 8Y, \tab using aliased entry techniques [as practiced in other parts of \tab the basic P?S/8 system] could lead to a more general set of \tab command capabilities somewhat similar to an implementation of \tab\b CCL\b0 .  System program such as MOVE, COPY, RENAME and DELETE could \tab be added to take advantage of the filtering and wild-card \tab capabilities of FILMAN.  [Note: This technique would be analogous \tab to that used within the DIRECT utility, also introduced in P?S/8 \tab Version 8Y.  Since these commands are actually program names, \tab exact names must be used unless six characters long; a true \b CCL \tab\b0 implementation would allow shorter commands as long as the \tab commands were unique.]\par
\par
\tab As part of any design, all contemplated commands must not be in \tab conflict with existing keyboard monitor and integrated editor \tab commands.  [Note: When active development of P?S/8 was put on \tab hiatus with the release of Version 8Z, these file-oriened \tab commands were an open issue regarding future implementation. \tab Since the P?S/8 SHELL overlay will address many of the same \tab issues for an entirely new environment, a byproduct of this new \tab development effort may lead to implementation of the commands as \tab discussed here.]\page\cf1 Appendix B [Continued].\cf0\par
\par
12)  The TY\{pe\} command was used in the R-L Monitor System to output \tab the contents of a file to the system console terminal.  This \tab command was also used in the 1976 release of P?S/8 Version 8P.  \tab For increased compatibility with DIBOL-8 and COS-300/310, the \tab command was changed to the LI\{st\} command [which is reminiscent \tab of various BASIC implementations which use the same command].\par
\par
     However, in many \b CCL\b0 -oriented command processors used in other \tab operating systems, LI\{st\} is associated with directing output to \tab the system line printer.  This issue will be addressed in the \tab design and implementation of the P?S/8 SHELL overlay.  It is \tab conceivable that the design intention in COS-300/310 was to use a \tab single command for both operations with an option switch [/T] to \tab force output to the system console printer; this form of command \tab syntax is incompatible with the OS/8 implementation of CCL which \tab is unexpected.  The P?S/8 SHELL will create its own set of \tab commands to serve the widest possible user community.  [Slavish \tab compatibility with any one system is not the primary goal; \tab improved utility and performance over other PDP-8 operating \tab systems is the main concern.]\par
\par
13)\tab The SE\{quence\} command was changed to the LN\{umber\} command for \tab increased compatibility with DIBOL-8 and COS-300/310.  An \tab immediate improvement was obtained; the program used to alter\tab\tab many keyboard monitor [and related] settings had been forced to \tab be named VARY because this implementation had been deferred; the \tab utility was renamed in P?S/8 Version 8U to SET.\par
\par
14)  At various points in the P?S/8 development effort, a \tab consideration was raised as to whether the RU\{n\} command would \tab have distinctive meaning from the R command.\par
\par
     Some of these distinctions have to do with the level of \tab implementation of extended-length files.  At one point, an entire \tab extended-length file directory structure was completely designed; \tab however, only manual entry of a single file was ever implemented.\par
\par
     The manner in which the present R and RU\{n\} commands are \tab implemented merely causes some additional swap overhead over the \tab compact subset of \b CCL\b0  available in newer of P?S/8; this allows \tab additional entries in the system programs directory where the \tab ability to execute a program by the use of the compact execution \tab form is either superfluous or not possible due to potential \tab anomaly with keyboard monitor and integrated line editor \tab commands.  No aspect of the in-memory environment is lost with \tab the possible exception of the edit buffer.  Thus, these commands \tab are only required to access programs in the system programs \tab directory which are unrelated to the keyboard monitor environment \tab [such as device-specific formatting and utility programs].\page\cf1 Appendix B [Continued].\cf0\par
\par
     Moreover, these commands must be avoided for successful use of \tab keyboard monitor extension utilities such as FIND and CHANGE\tab  \tab [which are only useful when used with the edit buffer fully \tab intact].\par
\par
     One possible variant [which was never implemented] was that the \tab scope of the RU\{n\} command would be increased to allow creation \tab of multiple extended-length files within the extended-length \tab directory.  [Note: As of the release of P?S/8 Version 8Z, this \tab structure is still present although inactive.]\par
\par
     With the recent development of the P?S/8 SHELL overlay, all such \tab plans have been abandoned in favor of the far more general \tab variable-length file environment of the SHELL directory \tab structure.  As such, the artifacts of the extended-length file \tab directory will be removed. The functionality of allowing one \tab extended-length file per logical unit will be retained; however, \tab the entire directory structure will be replaced with a small \tab reserved area at the end of the system programs directory [beyond \tab all system program entries].  [Note: All execution-class commands \tab require the system programs directory be read into memory to \tab lookup the desired program; as such, the extended-length file \tab entry will also be loaded at the same time reducing overhead as \tab compared with previous releases.]\par
\par
     Appropriate changes to the SET command will be made to allow \tab custom naming of the extended files on each logical unit to suit \tab the user's preference.  There will be no operational difference \tab between the R and RU[n] command.\par
\par
\tab As of this writing, Version 8Z has been released with several \tab binary patches to fix known bugs; all current work is focused on \tab the release of Version 9A some time in the future, which will \tab have the extended-length directory removed as described above.\par
\par
\tab Presently, only utilities such as the OS8CON utility are capable \tab of writing extended-length files using a work-around as described \tab later in Appendix B.  All system programs can access these files \tab for read purposes.\par
\par
15)\tab The LO\{ad\} command was changed to FE\{tch\} in subsequent releases \tab of P?S/8 [Version 8U and following] for increased compatibility \tab with DIBOL-8 and COS-300/310.  While this does make possible a \tab LOAD alias name for the BIN utility, this was never implemented.\par
     The GET alias name is the implemented program name for this \tab function.  It is trivial to either change the alias name or allow \tab both variants with identical meaning.\page\cf1 Appendix B [Continued].\cf3\f2\par
\cf0\f0\par
16)\tab The SA\{ve\} command was changed to WR\{ite\} in subsequent releases \tab of P?S/8 [Version 8U and following] for increased compatibility \tab with DIBOL-8 and COS-300/310.  It is possible a SAVE utility can \tab be created to transfer the image of memory obtained after a GET \tab program execution to a system program image and execution name; \tab however, there is no guarantee the appropriate storage blocks are \tab set aside on any particular system.  As such, this must be \tab deferred until a utility can be written to maintain all other \tab structures further up the system device [such as the starting \tab block of the TFS directory and extended-length file starting \tab block].  [Note: Due to the powerful nature of the P?S/8 system \tab programs directory, a practical SAVE utility must define overlay \tab structures loaded into extended memory if relevant.  Program \tab execution may allow operation on a 4K system, but creation of the \tab complete program image will require extended memory unless an \tab alternate facility is created that works with binary files, not \tab the contents of memory.]\par
\par
\tab There are plans to create a utility with a wider scope to also \tab include the ability to create a working copy of P?S/8 from \tab various binary resources without the need to assemble the source \tab files.  Once such a utility completely manages the storage device \tab more effectively, it will be a fairly easy matter to create a \tab SAVE utility as described above.\par
\par
\tab [Note: Despite the lack of a SAVE utility, P?S/8 currently \tab provides ample facility to deal with core-image programs.  P?S/8 \tab ODT operates on the atest memory image after a GET command; from \tab within ODT it is possible to start any program as if it were a \tab system program without restriction.  The START utility can pass \tab all parameters with several slight restrictions regarding s\tab tarting address and memory field.  The BSAVE utility can save \tab arbitrary memory locations and memory ranges anywhere in 32K \tab memory into a stream of binary \i slurp\i0  files.  As of Version 8X of \tab P?S/8, the R and RU\{n] commands can run the latest memory image \tab by specifying either % or $ as the execution name.; the only \tab restriction is the program must start at 00200, the usual \tab starting address of most PDP-8 programs.]\par
\par
17)\tab There were intentions to expand the RU\{n\} command to fully \tab support the extended-length file directory including multiple \tab variable-length files.  Had this been implemented, a distinction \tab would have to be drawn between the R and the RU[n] commands.  In \tab addition, the extended RU\{n\} command would have violated the \tab memory layout of the keyboard monitor regarding the edit buffer \tab and most of the operational code.  This would be necessary to \tab load additional support code into memory; a design decision would \tab be required as to whether to allow the edit buffer to be \tab destroyed or alternatively always saving the edit buffer to \tab either the $ or % positional files.  A command-processing error \tab in such an environment would require an entire system reload as \tab well as cause additional overhead.\page\cf1 Appendix B [Continued].\cf0\par
\par
\tab [Note: Independently of these considerations, the SET command \tab suports enabling automatic saving of the edit buffer into the $ \tab file on the system device and a companion option to load the \tab editing buff from the $ file on the system device upon any \tab execution-class command; neither of these options are mandatory \tab and one does not depend on the other in any way.]\par
\par
\tab The original objective was the RU\{n\} command could create \tab extended-length files that once closed could be read by the R \tab command as it is presently implemented.\par
\par
\tab With the development of the P?S/8 SHELL overlay, this entire line \tab of system development has been abandoned; the SHELL will \tab implement a significantly more functional environment [which can \tab be enabled or disabled with a single command].  As appropriate, \tab conversion programs will be provided to migrate extended-length \tab files to the SHELL environment,\par
\par
18)\tab Due to the nature of the modifications regarding support for the \tab new definition of the extended-length file [with only a single \tab extended-length file per logical device unit], a temporary \tab restriction exists with regard to access to the extended-length \tab file until these changes are implemented in the next release.  A \tab viable workaround procedure will be presented.\par
\par
\tab Extended-length files are created by programs such as OS8CON and \tab L6DCON when appropriate options are used to distinguish extended-\tab length output file format from the that used with TFS output \tab files.\par
\par
\tab When fully implemented, a level of safety will be implemented to \tab ensure passed output files are actually in the extended-length \tab file area; however, for the present these conversion programs do \tab not check for this.  This was made necessary because it is not \tab possible to specify extended-length output files presently.\par
\par
\tab The user is advised to create extended-length files carefully by \tab specifying the highest block file within the TFS directory.  \tab [Note: When the actual highest possible TFS file block is used \tab for this purpose, the value is exactly 16 blocks before the \tab extended-length entry in the proper directory format would be.  \tab As such, as long as the user ensures the proper placement for the \tab target TFS output files, these utilities can be used with \tab impunity.\par
\par
\tab When the next release is completed, the storage device can be \tab configured for proper block utilization; extended files created \tab using the workaround can be converted with OS8CON and then \tab converted back to proper blocks.  [Note: The restriction does not \tab affect the operation of OS8CON with regard to converting P?S/8 \tab files to OS/8 data.]\page\cf1 Appendix B [Continued].\cf3\f2\par
