\cf3 Appendix C Notes.\cf0\par
\par
 1)\tab Certain terminals are capable of generating an eight-bit \tab character code, especially Teletype models 33 ASR and 35 ASR, \tab which have attached paper-tape readers.  While the high-order bit \tab of the eight-bit data might have meaning in the paper-tape \tab system, P?S/8 universally strips off the high-order bit; as such, \tab there are only seven significant bits in the character code set \tab for consideration.\par
\par
\tab In all contexts, the parity bit is ignored.  [Note: Various P?S/8 \tab utilities are designed to import/export from/to paper-tape \tab devices; where applicable, the parity bit will always be set to \tab conform to paper-tape mark parity conventions.]\page\cf1 Appendix C Notes [Continued].\cf0\par
\par
 2)\tab\highlight3 <NUL>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 <BRK>\highlight0 ] and \highlight3 <RO>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 <DEL>\highlight0 ] are \tab used as part of timeout schemes for various output devices such \tab as the standard device 66 line printer or device 02 high-speed \tab punch.  These optional devices may not be present in the current \tab configuration; alternatively, the operator may have placed one of \tab the devices in an offline state to temporarily prevent access.  \tab While no one method applies in all circumstances, these codes are \tab generally chosen as part of the device initialization; the \tab outputted data is usually ignored.\par
\par
 3)\tab Many PDP-8 programs support some form of system console keyboard-\tab based forced exit stategy.  The most widely used exit code is \tab\highlight3 <ETX>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 C\highlight0 ] which is often printed as \cf3\f2 ^C\cf0\f0  by \tab the program's exit monitoring routines or the operating system \tab upon detection.\par
\par
\tab Within the P?S/8 operating system, the system BATCH facility \tab will abort the current sequence if \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 C\highlight0  is detected.  \tab However, if a program exits to the operating system using the \tab standard restart address of 07600, BATCH abort may be avoided by \tab the use of alternate exit characters.  Many P?S/8 system programs \tab check for the additional codes \highlight3 <NUL>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 <BRK>\highlight0 ], \highlight3 <SOH>\highlight0  \tab [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 A\highlight0  and sometimes printed as \cf3\f2 ^A\cf0\f0 ] and \highlight3 <STX>\highlight0  \tab [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 B\highlight0  and sometimes printed as \cf3\f2 ^B\cf0\f0 ].  Since \tab the P?S/8 keyboard monitor checks explicitly for \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 C\highlight0 , the \tab latest program can exit without aborting the overall BATCH \tab operation that may be in effect.\par
\par
 4)\tab\highlight3 SOH>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 A\highlight0 ] is used within the keyboard \tab monitor ED\{it\} command to initiate an additional search for the \tab character located in the previous search operation.\par
\par
 5)\tab\highlight3 <EOT>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 D\highlight0 ] was used to end auto-sequenced \tab text input in the P?S/8 keyboard monitor prior to Version 8Y.  In \tab newer releases, this function is performed by \highlight3 <SUB>\highlight0  [also known \tab as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 Z\highlight0 ] to conform with existing industry standards for \tab end of medium.\par
\par
 6)\tab\highlight3 <ENQ>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 E\highlight0 ] is not used in P?S/8.  When \tab running P?S/8 from the SIMH PDP-8 simulator, \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 E\highlight0  is used \tab to exit the simulator after closing all open devices.\par
\par
 7)\tab\highlight3 <ACK>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 F\highlight0 ] is used within the keyboard \tab monitor ED\{it\} command to print out any characters remaining in \tab the buffer to allow either further editing or closing out the \tab line being edited.\page\cf1 Appendix C Notes [Continued].\cf0\par
\par
 8)\tab\highlight3 <BEL>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 G\highlight0 ] is used within the keyboard \tab monitor ED\{it\} command to initiate a search for a character in \tab the buffer.  All characters up to and including a match between \tab the next character typed and the remaining contents of the buffer \tab will be displayed; further editing operations are allowed \tab including additional search operations.\par
\par
\tab Each time the keyboard monitor is reloaded, a \highlight3 <BEL>\highlight0  character is \tab sent to the system console terminal to indicate the system is \tab ready for further keyboard input.  Future releases of the P?S/8 \tab SET utility will include the command SET SYSTEM [NO] BEEP to \tab manage this action.\par
\par
 9)\tab Within the keyboard monitor, the \i rubout\i0  [delete previous \tab character] function can be initiated by several characters \tab including the \highlight3 <BS>\highlight0  character on the system console keyboard [if \tab present] and the \highlight3 <RO>\highlight0  key [often labeled \cf2\highlight1\f2 RUBOUT\cf0\highlight0\f0 ].  When the PDP-8 \tab is being simulated on various PC systems, there is often an \tab enlarged key with a legend consisting of a left-facing arrow \tab [often labeled \cf2\highlight1\f2 Backspace\cf0\highlight0\f0 ].  The actual code generated within the \tab simulated environment could be either \highlight3 <BS>\highlight0  or \highlight3 <RO>\highlight0 ; most PDP-8 \tab programs handle either code as initiating the \i rubout\i0  function as \tab described above.\par
\par
\tab The \highlight3 <BS>\highlight0  character is often used on video terminals as part of \tab the support for the the rubout function.  Most video terminals \tab will visually delete and backup the cursor if the sequence \highlight3 <BS>\highlight0  \tab\highlight3 <SP>\highlight0  \highlight3 <BS>\highlight0  is sent to the terminal.  However, certain devices such \tab as the VT05, VT52 and VT100 are supported using custom character \tab sequences particular to each terminal.  Additional information \tab about the P?S/8 SET utility is available in other documents.\par
\par
10)\tab\highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  is used to align text to boundaries of eight columns in many \tab DEC systems as well as MS-DOS, Unix and Windows.  The specific \tab interpretation of the \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  character in the keyboard monitor is \tab determined by the TA\{b\} command which can be set to accommodate a \tab wide variety of system console terminals.  The default setting is \tab used for support of the \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  on devices such as the Teletype \tab Model 33 which does not support the \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  character directly; \highlight3 <SP>\highlight0  \tab characters are output as necessary to obtain the desired visual \tab alignment.  The Teletype Model 35 is supported using another \tab setting particular to this device; generic support is available \tab for devices that support the \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  directly.  For rough draft \tab output on very slow devices, \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  support can be reduced to \tab always printing a single \highlight3 <SP>\highlight0  character.\par
\par
\tab For systems that are always configured for a specific terminal, \tab the SET utility can change the default setting to any of the \tab supported modes.  Additional information about the P?S/8 SET \tab utility is available in other documents.\page\cf1 Appendix C Notes [Continued].\cf0\par
\par
11)\tab The \highlight3 <LF>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 J\highlight0 ] character is used as part of \tab the \highlight3 <CR>\highlight0 /\highlight3 <LF>\highlight0  sequence required on all printed output and \tab supported by all system programs when outputting to the system \tab console terminal or system line printer.\par
\par
\tab Within the keyboard monitor, pressing \highlight3 <LF>\highlight0  will output \highlight3 <CR>\highlight0 /\highlight3 <LF>\highlight0  \tab followed by the tentative contents of the command-line buffer. \tab This is useful on hard copy terminals especially after multiple \tab rubout operations have occurred; a clear view of the tentative \tab command-line is often needed to better understand the command in \tab progress.\par
\par
\tab When paper-tapes of text data are read in on the readers of such \tab devices as the Teletype Model 33 ASR and Model 35 ASR, the text \tab often includes both \highlight3 <CR>\highlight0  and \highlight3 <LF>\highlight0  for each line of text; as such, \tab it is undesirable to allow the usual operation of the \highlight3 <LF>\highlight0  during \tab auto-sequenced text input operations as this will tend to produce \tab undesirable doubled output.  To prevent this, use the system \tab program command SET LNFEED IGNORE; the normal function of echoing \tab the current tentative command line can be restored by using the \tab system program command SET LNFEED VIEW.  Information about \tab addtional P?S/8 SET utility options is available in other \tab documents.\par
\par
12)\tab\highlight3 <VT>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 K\highlight0 ] is used within the keyboard \tab monitor ED\{it\} command to delete all characters in the command-\tab line buffer already processed and sent to the system console \tab printer.\par
\par
13)\tab The \highlight3 <FF>\highlight0  character [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 L\highlight0 ] is used on the \tab system line printer to separate physical pages of output; all \tab printer must support this function properly.\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 text files do not support the \highlight3 <FF>\highlight0  character directly; \tab however, P?S/8 PAL supports the EJECT directive which will output \tab a \highlight3 <FF>\highlight0  causing further output to be placed on the next page.  \tab [The statement which includes the EJECT directive and an optional \tab argument is not displayed within the assembly listing.  The \tab optional argument, if present, is used to change the title of the \tab assembly listing on the next printed page in a manner similar to \tab the TITLE directive.]\par
\par
\tab The prevailing conversion programs [such as P?S/8 OS8CON] will \tab convert the character sequence \highlight3 <HT>\highlight0  EJECT \highlight3 <CR>\highlight0  to \highlight3 <FF>\highlight0  for use in \tab systems that support \highlight3 <FF>\highlight0  directly.  [Support of this function is \tab available in both directions of the conversion process.]\par
\par
14)\tab The \highlight3 <CR>\highlight0  character [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 M\highlight0 ] is used to \tab terminate all keyboard monitor commands.  Commands that insert \tab text lines into the edit buffer terminate the line internally \tab with 00.\page\cf1 Appendix C Notes [Continued].\cf0\par
\par
\tab There is an alignment consideration when terminating any text \tab line: If the \highlight3 <CR>\highlight0  is being applied by inserting 00 into the low-\tab order bits of the latest 12-bit word, no further changes are \tab required.  If the \highlight3 <CR>\highlight0  is being applied by inserting 00 into the \tab high-order 12-bits of the latest word, the low-order bits must be \tab set to a non-zero value to avoid confusion with the file text \tab terminator, which is a full 12-bit word of 0000.\par
\par
15)\tab\highlight3 <SO>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 N\highlight0 ] and \highlight3 <SI>\highlight0  [also known as \tab\highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 O\highlight0 ] are used on certain devices [such as the LA36 \tab DECWriter with the APL option installed] to change the operating \tab mode from ASCII to the APL character set.  Optional keycaps are \tab used on terminals that support this option to assist the user as \tab the two operating modes are significantly different.  [The \tab DataSouth DS-120 speedup board for the LA36 always includes APL \tab support.]\par
\par
16)\tab\highlight3 <SI>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 O\highlight0 ] is used in many P?S/8 printing \tab programs to invert the state of printing; this allows voluminous \tab output to be temporarily ignored; when \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 O\highlight0  is pressed a \tab second time, the output will contine [generally much further \tab along in the overall printing process].  However, it takes \tab practice to determine how long to wait between key pressings of \tab\highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 O\highlight0  to avoid total loss of the rest of the output.  The \tab primary purpose of the \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 O\highlight0  function is to effectively \tab cancel assembly listing accidently sent to the system console \tab instead of the system line printer.\par
\par
17)\tab\highlight3 <DLE>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 P\highlight0 ] is used in certain P?S/8 utility \tab programs to restart the utility generally abandoning the previous \tab command or operation.\par
\par
18)\tab\highlight3 <DC3>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 S\highlight0 ] and \highlight3 <DC1>\highlight0  [also known as \tab\highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 Q\highlight0 ] are used to pause and release all P?S/8 system \tab console output as part of the industry standard "XON/XOFF" \tab protocol.  Many DEC and DEC-compatible devices such as the VT-100 \tab and LA-120 require this protocol for proper operation.  Various \tab serial line printer devices which use the XON/XOFF protocol are \tab also supported by the P?S/8 Logical Console Overlay [when \tab suitably configured].\par
\par
\tab Very few operating systems for the PDP-8 fully support XON/XOFF \tab protocol.  All members of the DEC OS/8 family of operating \tab systems are incapable of this support at the software level with \tab the exception of partial support when output is directed to a \tab specific handler [generally known as KL8E].  However, all OS/8 \tab programs perform their own console handling completely devoid of \tab XON/XOFF support leading to garbled output on affected devices \tab unless all output is reduced to very low baud rates.\page\cf1 Appendix C Notes [Continued].\cf0\par
\par
\tab Instead of redesigning OS/8, DECmate models were released with \tab the ability of the internal terminal emulator to prevent buffer \tab overrun without the use of any protocol; however, OS/8 family \tab members [such as OS/278] cannot provide support for the PAUSE key \tab or any other serial console device without the same problems as \tab all other similar systems.\par
\par
19)\tab\highlight3 <DC2>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 R\highlight0 ] and \highlight3 <DC4>\highlight0  [also known as \tab\highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 T\highlight0 ] are used respectively to rewind and unload the \tab current system device.  Support is hardware-dependent; both \tab commands may perform the same operation or no operation for \tab certain hardware configurations.\par
\par
\tab On the P?S/8 system for TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56, pressing \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 R\highlight0  \tab while entering any command or other keyboard input on the system \tab console device will rewind the current system device unit.  \tab Control is immediately returned to the keyboard monitor; as such, \tab commands may be typed while the DECtape is rewinding.  This can \tab be useful when an execution-class command is being entered, as \tab the DECtape blocks required to process the command are located \tab near the beginning of the DECtape.  The tape is automatically \tab stopped by the DECtape controller when the reverse end zone is \tab detected.\par
\par
\tab Pressing \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 T\highlight0  on the same configuration will rewind the \tab system device unit; however, the DECtape status is continuously \tab monitored until the end zone is detected.  Additional commands \tab are issued to force the DECtape off the takeup reel.  If the \tab DECtape drive is a TU55, the brakes are locked stopping the \tab motors; TU56 drives will require manual intervention by placing \tab the drive off-line.\par
\par
20)\tab\highlight3 <NAK>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 U\highlight0 ] is used to cancel the latest \tab command or other input to the keyboard monitor.  When used during \tab the keyboard monitor ED\{it\} command, \highlight3 Control\f2\fs29 -\f0\fs24 U\highlight0  completely \tab restarts the editing process with the original line contents.\par
\par
\tab When used in auto-sequenced text input mode, the latest line \tab number is repeated; all input on the current line is discarded.\par
\par
21)\tab\highlight3 <ESC>\highlight0  [also known as \highlight3 <ALT>\highlight0 ] is used to terminate all input to the \tab\tab keyboard monitor and in most contexts is equivalent to \tab\tab\tab typing \highlight3 <CR>\highlight0  with the following exceptions:\par
\par
\tab a)\tab Typing \highlight3 <CR>\highlight0  will terminate the line and output \highlight3 <CR>\highlight0 /\highlight3 <LF>\highlight0  to \tab\tab start another line in the usual manner that applies.  Typing \tab\tab\highlight3 <ESC>\highlight0  will output \cf2\highlight1\f2 $\cf0\highlight0\f0  only.\page\cf1 Appendix C Notes [Continued].\cf0\par
\par
\tab b)\tab Certain programs are designed to scan the command line by \tab\tab\tab setting special scanning restrictions within the keyboard \tab\tab\tab monitor.  When the command line is terminated by typing \tab\tab\tab\highlight3 <ESC>\highlight0  the program may change its operating mode [generally \tab\tab\tab to imply program exit after performing the specific command \tab\tab passed on the command line].  Such usage only applies to \tab\tab\tab programs designed to scan the command line and operate \tab\tab\tab either repeatedly or once-only depending on the command line\par
\tab\tab termination character.\par
\par
\tab On certain terminals, the \highlight3 <ESC>\highlight0  function may be provided by a key \tab marked as \cf2\highlight1\f2 ALT MODE\cf0\highlight0\f0  [or perhaps \cf2\highlight1\f2 ALT\cf0\highlight0\f0 ].  The actual character code \tab generated by pressing this key might be either 175 octal or 176 \tab octal depending on exact terminal characteristics.  The \tab disposition of the \highlight3 <ESC>\highlight0  function and the exact keyboard code \tab used on any particular console device can be configured with the \tab P?S/8 SET utility.  Additional information about the P?S/8 SET \tab utility is available in other documents.\par
\par
22)\tab P?S/8 text files do not support \cf5\f2 @\cf0\f0  or \cf5\f2 `\cf0\f0  [the latter is supported \tab in some systems as the lower-case equivalent of the former].\par
\par
\tab In the full definition of the PDP-8 Assembly Language [PAL] \tab specifications, the only valid use of the \cf5\f2 @\cf0\f0  character is as a \tab variant delimiter character within TEXT statements; however, \tab many other characters are available to serve the same purpose.  \tab The only requirement of a delimiter character is to frame the \tab text string consisting of valid text characters other than the \tab delimiter character; as such, there should never be any \tab problematic cases of TEXT statements that cannot be stated using \tab one of the other valid delimiter characters.\par
\par
\tab Recent releases of P?S/8 PAL support this ability to use ordinary \tab printing characters as delimiter characters in TEXT statements.  \tab As such, the problem of delimiter substitution is somewhat easier \tab to solve than in previous releases.\par
\par
\tab Since no implementation of the PAL language allows lower-case \tab input within statements, the \f2\fs16 `\f0\fs24  is not a valid delimiter of TEXT \tab statements.\line\line\tab The latest version of the inter-system file conversion programs \tab such as OS8CON, \i L6DCON\i0  and the soon to be released PTUTIL utility \tab will implement conversion of both the \cf5\f2 @ and \cf0\fs16 `\f0\fs24  characters to the # \tab character.  This is an acceptable substitution since the only \tab allowed usage for these characters could be as text string \tab delimiters such as are used with the TEXT directive and related \tab assembler features.  [Note: In the event this is in conflict with \tab the text string contents, manual editing will be required.]\page\tab\cf1 Appendix C Notes [Continued].\par
\par
\cf0\tab Note: The P?S/8 SHELL will support an advanced assembler based on \tab P?S/8 PAL [tentatively named XPAL] which will support the seven-\tab bit character set.  Character set rules of this implementation \tab will be greatly expanded compared to all other known versions of \tab PAL.\par
\par
