25)\tab The character code for the \highlight3 <ESC>\highlight0  key is generally 27 decimal [033 \tab octal [1B hex] and typically labeled \cf2\highlight1\f2 ESC\cf0\highlight0\f0  ; however, very early \tab production Teletype Model 33 and Model 35 terminals use a key \tab labeled \cf2\highlight1\f2 ALT MODE\cf0\highlight0\f0  [or \cf2\highlight1\f2 ALT\cf0\highlight0\f0  in some cases].  Certain production \tab models were used on PDP-8 systems where the code generated by \tab pressing this key is 125 decimal [175 octal] [7D hex] or 126 \tab decimal [175 octal] [7E hex].\par
\par
\tab Various third-party maintenance companies were contracted by \tab certain PDP-8 users unsatisfied with the generally poor quality \tab of DEC field service, especially with regard to Teletype \tab products.  Many of the employees of these service companies were \tab experts who had worked for the Teletype corporation before their \tab position as field technicians.  Some of them were known to offer \tab custom modifications to these terminals for an additional service \tab fee [or perhaps as a personal service].  As such, virtually every \tab combination of keycap legend and generated code described in this \tab document were to be found on certain of these terminals.\par
\par
\tab Users of LINC-8 systems who preferred to use PDP-8 software \tab instead of primarily LINC-oriented software often paid to have \tab the entire set of keys replaced as well as the original typewheel \tab which printed the earliest of the various LINC character sets \tab that were supported over the years.  DEC supplied keys with \tab legends of very early LINC Assembly Program [LAP] systems while \tab the prevailing software developers were simultaneously changing \tab the character sets displayed on the screen.  As such, key legends \tab and typewheel characters could not keep up with which particular \tab LAP-type system was in vogue.\par
\par
\tab Some of the LAP-type systems included converson programs to \tab upgrade existing source programs to be compatible with the newer \tab releases.  With no central standard to conform to, it was \tab inevitable that replacement keys with different legends would be \tab needed as well as replacement typewheels that were in compliance \tab with the particular LAP system in use.  With the expertise of the \tab technicians available, particular users could generally stay in \tab sync with the needs of the software systems they embraced.\par
\par
\tab Eventually, the newest LAP-type systems embraced the ASCII \tab character set rendering all prior generations of key legends and \tab typewheels obsolete.  In the end, users of PDP-8 software and \tab LINC-oriented software generally agreed to use the more typical \tab ASCII key legends and typewheels.\page\cf1 Appendix C Notes [Continued].\par
\cf0\par
\tab For specific users who insisted on obsolescent personal \tab preference, handmade legend strips were created and glued onto \tab the terminal adjacent to the corresponding keys to aid in \tab deciphering the replaced keys to maintain their former meaning; \tab since the LINC-coded typewheel had been converted to an ASCII-\tab coded typewheel, it was impossible to print the former LINC \tab characters [assuming a match to the particulars of the user's \tab preferred LAP version].\par
\par
\tab When a Teletype Model 33 ASR is encountered as a LINC-8 console \tab terminal, it is possible the key legend for the \highlight3 <ESC>\highlight0  key is \tab marked \cf2\highlight1\f2 META\cf0\highlight0\f0  for cosmetic compatibility with the original LINC \tab computer regardless of the actual code generated when the key is \tab pressed.\par
\par
\tab P?S/8 supports a designated code for \highlight3 <ESC>\highlight0  which defaults to 033 \tab octal.  The SET utility program can be used to change the \tab designated code to conform to local requirements.  As long as the \tab code for \highlight3 <ESC>\highlight0  is not customized to 175 octal or 176 octal, these \tab character codes are available to the keyboard monitor for their \tab designated purpose and will be converted to the upper-case \tab equivalent.\par
\par
\tab The disposition of certain characters as described here is for \tab the basic P?S/8 system as of Version Z.  Future releases will \tab include the P?S/8 SHELL overlay; files created in this \tab environment will support nearly the entire seven-bit character \tab set [and perhaps certain extensions].\par
