\b BIN\par
\par
\b0\tab\tab The DEC standard paper-tape format used for most binary file \tab\tab loading from the stand-alone environment defined as the CPU, \tab\tab the system console terminal and a paper-tape reader.  The \tab\tab\tab reader may be either a high-speed reader [device code 02] or \tab\tab part of the system console [generally a Teletype Model 33 \tab\tab\tab ASR or Model 35 ASR].\page\cf1 Glossary of terms used in this document [continued].\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 implements a compatible version of the \b BIN \b0 loader \tab\tab\tab that can be loaded into the last page of memory [07600-\tab\tab\tab 07777] along with the \b RIM\b0  loader as is generally the case \tab\tab\tab with other versions of similar programs.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab Note: There are several known implementations of the \b BIN \tab\tab\tab\b0 loader, all of which are designed to occupy some portion of \tab\tab 07600-0777; due the differences in exact loading, the \tab\tab\tab\tab starting address is defined as 07777 which is actually a JMP \tab\tab instruction referencing the internal starting address to \tab\tab\tab avoid the need for version-specific documentation.\par
\par
\tab\tab The P?S/8 implementation adds additional functionality; when \tab\tab the \b BIN\b0  and \b RIM\b0  loaders are in memory, a standalone \tab\tab\tab\tab bootstrap for the current P?S/8 configuration is loaded into \tab\tab 07600-07642.  P?S/8 can be reloaded by starting the computer \tab\tab at the standard restart address of 07600.  The only \tab\tab\tab\tab requirements for a successful reload are: a) The data field \tab\tab is reset to 00, b) None of the locations in the range of \tab\tab\tab 07600-07642 are modified.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: There are a small number of known "rogue" programs \tab\tab\tab that violate this convention;  some can actually destroy \tab\tab\tab specific implementations of the \b BIN\b0  loader due to poor \tab\tab\tab design and expectation of the memory layout of a particular \tab\tab implementation of the \b BIN\b0  loader which is contrary to all \tab\tab\tab known PDP-8 programming conventions.  The only recourse \tab\tab\tab after loading one of these programs is to manually reload \tab\tab\tab memory to repair the program corruption; however, most of \tab\tab\tab these programs do not violate the \b RIM\b0  loader memory layout. \tab\tab All implementations of the \b RIM\b0  loader are identical; as \tab\tab\tab such, copies of the contents of 07600-07754 can be punched \tab\tab\tab in \b RIM\b0  format to restore memory as needed.\par
\par
\tab\tab All standalone implementations of the \b BIN\b0  loader use the \tab\tab\tab front panel switch register to select choice of reader \tab\tab\tab hardware.  The P?S/8 implementation of \b BIN\b0  and\b  RIM\b0  includes \tab\tab preconfiguration by the use of option switches; as such, the \tab\tab front panel does not affect either program. [Note: Not all \tab\tab\tab machines support a front panel.]  The P?S/8 system program \tab\tab\tab BIN [not to be confused with \b BIN\b0 ] is used to load the \tab\tab\tab\tab standalone bootstrap, the \b BIN \b0 loader and the \b RIM \b0 loader via \tab\tab the following command:\par
\par
\tab\tab .BIN /P /F /R \cf3 or (PFR)\cf0\par
\par
\tab\tab /P must be used to indicate the paper-tape loaders will be \tab\tab\tab loaded into memory [instead of the contents of \i slurp\i0  binary \tab\tab files].\page\cf1 Glossary of terms used in this document [continued].\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab /F can be optionally specified to indicate the choice of the \tab\tab high-speed reader, which is applied to both \b BIN and RIM\b0 ; if \tab\tab left out the low-speed reader is used in both loaders.\par
\par
\tab\tab /R can be optionally specified to indicate the \b RIM \b0 loader \tab\tab\tab will be started; the default is to start the \b BIN\b0  loader.\par
