Core image [or core-image when used as an adjective].\par
\par
\tab\tab A Core image refers to data stored on blocks of storage \tab\tab\tab where entire pages of program or other data are presented in \tab\tab 128 word units meant to be loaded into various memory \tab\tab\tab\tab locations.  Specific loading of groups of core-image\par
\tab\tab blocks is determined outside of the core-image data such as \tab\tab within directory or other complex file structures that \tab\tab\tab manage loading and mapping issues.\par
\par
\tab\tab Core images stored in order of adjacency by loading address \tab\tab are the most efficient form of direct binary storage for \tab\tab\tab executable programs assuming the operating system maintains \tab\tab relevant loading parameters [such as the P?S/8 system \tab\tab\tab\tab programs directory].\par
\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 system programs are stored in core-image form for at \tab\tab\tab least the first load segment of fully adjacent storage \tab\tab\tab blocks; additional segments may be defined by the program \tab\tab\tab for additional purposes such as program or data\par
\tab\tab overlays.\par
\par
\tab\tab\i Slurp-\i0 format binary files are not core-image files; however, \tab\tab\i slurp-\i0 format binary files can be loaded into memory and\tab\tab\tab saved into system blocks 0020-0057 which is a core image of\par
\tab\tab all of field 0.  Once saved, various utilities exist to \tab\tab\tab interact with the core image saved in these blocks which are \tab\tab also known as the system scratch area.  For more \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab information, see the definition of the % and $ files. Copies \tab\tab of these files can be saved to named TFS files if desired.  \tab\tab Restoring the % and $ files to their former contents will \tab\tab\tab restore the core image to its former state; this allows \tab\tab\tab additional work to be performed on an ongoing project \tab\tab\tab\tab perhaps undergoing debugging with P?S/8 ODT.\par
\par
\tab\tab While P?S/8 does not currently provide a means to directly \tab\tab\tab create core-image system programs, the current core-image \tab\tab\tab can be run using either the R [or RU\{n\}] % or R [or RU\{n\}] $ \tab\tab command; all of the options of typical P?S/8 system programs \tab\tab are available except for the possibility of internal \tab\tab\tab\tab overlays.   A thorough knowledge of P?S/8 is required to \tab\tab\tab create complex system programs on a permanent basis.  Only \tab\tab\tab advanced users will be technically qualified to take \tab\tab\tab\tab advantage of the overlay facility inherent in the system \tab\tab\tab programs directory.\page\cf1 Glossary of terms used in this document [continued].\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab A complete understanding of the internal workings of P?S/8 \tab\tab\tab execution and interaction with the system programs directory \tab\tab and the system device handler are necessary prerequisites \tab\tab\tab for a successful system program project.  Advanced \tab\tab\tab\tab information on this topic will be made available in separate \tab\tab documents covering P?S/8 principles of operation and other \tab\tab\tab information.  [Note: Once these topics are mastered, the \tab\tab\tab ability to create any form of permanent system program will \tab\tab be trivial and quite obvious; however, future releases of\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 will provide utilities to assist in the system program \tab\tab creation process.]\par
