P?S\par
\par
\tab\tab The Poly Question Society [P?S] is the self-appointed name \tab\tab\tab of a group of questioners most of whom met at the school \tab\tab\tab affectionately known as Brooklyn Poly or Brooklyn Polytech \tab\tab\tab or the Polytechnic Institute of Brooklyn.  Various other \tab\tab\tab names of the school have existed over the years; as of the \tab\tab\tab time of this writing, the school has been taken over by New \tab\tab York University [NYU] and many people have many names for \tab\tab\tab this institution, some of which are not necessarily suitable \tab\tab for publication or even discussion in mixed company.\par
\par
\tab\tab Most of the members of the P?S [whether living, dead, or \tab\tab\tab mythological creatures] are additionally somewhat legendary \tab\tab in the annals of computer programming [and in some cases \tab\tab\tab beyond the limitations of their own minds] and are \tab\tab\tab\tab responsible for many programming projects, most of which \tab\tab\tab were eventually products of Digital Equipment Corporation \tab\tab\tab [DEC].\par
\par
\tab\tab When and where possible, P?S should be spelled \i P?S\i0  to be \tab\tab\tab pronounced as pee-kew-ess; If necessary, it can be printed \tab\tab\tab as PQS which corresponds to the pronounciation.\par
