RIM\par
\par
\tab\tab\b0 This is the alternate standard DEC format for binary paper-\tab\tab\tab tape loading.  \b RIM\b0  format is used primarily to load in the \tab\tab\tab\b BIN\b0  loader or other short binary program.  It is less \tab\tab\tab\tab versatile than \b BIN\b0  format; however the loader is much \tab\tab\tab\tab shorter and can be entered manually from the front panel of \tab\tab most PDP-8 systems.\par
\par
\tab\tab [Note: \b RIM\b0  format binary paper-tapes are longer than \b BIN\b0  \tab\tab\tab format binary paper-tapes because automatic increment of the \tab\tab loading address is not supported; each memory location must \tab\tab include both an address as well as the contents even if the \tab\tab loading happens to be sequential.  Field settings are not \tab\tab\tab supported; as such, program loading is limited to a single \tab\tab\tab memory field.  The data field for program loading must be \tab\tab\tab set manually on the front panel.  Most importantly, \b RIM\b0  \tab\tab\tab format does not support data checksumming.  This is the \tab\tab\tab principal reason why \b RIM\b0  format is only recommended for \tab\tab\tab loading short programs such as the \b BIN\b0  loader.]\par
\par
\tab\tab Most PDP-8 storage-based operating systems can be started \tab\tab\tab using a bootup program even smaller than the \b RIM \b0 loader; as \tab\tab such, given a choice, it is generally less manual effort to \tab\tab reload P?S/8 than to toggle in the \b RIM \b0 loader.  For certain \tab\tab configurations, the reverse is true; in those instances, the \tab\tab\b RIM \b0 loader presents a better choice for computer startup \tab\tab\tab [assuming a paper-tape reader is available].\page\cf1 Glossary of terms used in this document [continued].\cf3\par
