\i Slurp\par
\par
\tab\tab\i0 This is the name given to the P?S/8 binary file format by \tab\tab\tab its inventor, Richard Lary, coauthor of the R-L Monitor \tab\tab\tab System, which is the precursor system P?S/8 is partially \tab\tab\tab based on.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab\i Slurp\i0  binary format is the most efficient format for loading \tab\tab random access binary files into memory from a storage \tab\tab\tab\tab device.  Actual loading efficiency approaches 5/6 that of \tab\tab\tab block-oriented [core-image] binary format while allowing the \tab\tab loading of individual locations or large blocks of \tab\tab\tab\tab contiguous memory without requiring any particular block \tab\tab\tab structure.  Memory can be freely loaded anywhere in the \tab\tab\tab entire PDP-8 32K memory space [other than system reserved \tab\tab\tab memory].\par
\par
\tab\tab Most configurations of P?S/8 support a standalone \i slurp\b\i0  \tab\tab\tab\b0 loader that displaces the system device handler.  The \tab\tab\tab\tab standalone loader includes the same system restart code as\tab\tab\tab defined in the description of the P?S/8 variant of the \b BIN\b0  \tab\tab\tab loader elsewhere in this document.\par
\par
\tab\tab The P?S/8 BIN program initiates the \i slurp \i0 loader passing a \tab\tab\tab list of \i slurp\i0 -format binary files specified by the user for \tab\tab loading using a command such as the following:\par
\par
\tab\tab .BIN \i list of input files\i0  /H /Z /I /V \cf3 or (HZIv)\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab /H can be optionally specified to indicate all non-reserved \tab\tab memory will be filled with 7402 [the PDP-8 HLT instruction] \tab\tab before the contents of the input files are loaded.\par
\par
\tab\tab /Z can be optionally specified to indicate all non-reserved \tab\tab memory will be filled with 0000 [the LINC HLT instruction] \tab\tab\tab before the contents of the input files are loaded.  [Note: \tab\tab\tab /H and /Z are mutually exclusive and cannot be used \tab\tab\tab\tab together.]\par
\par
\tab\tab /I can be optionally specified to indicate the system device \tab\tab handler should be automatically reloaded after the contents \tab\tab of the input files are loaded.  [Note: Normally as many as \tab\tab\tab 17 input files can be passed to the \i slurp\i0  loader; when /I is \tab\tab in effect the maximum number is lowered to 16 input files.]\par
\par
\tab\tab If /V is specified, the standalone \i slurp\i0  loader is not used. \tab\tab Instead, a virtual \i slurp\i0  loader is used which generally \tab\tab\tab takes longer to accomplish the same file loading.  If the \tab\tab\tab virtual \i slurp \i0 loader is in effect, the /I switch is ignored.  \tab\tab [Note: On systems that do not support the standalone \i slurp\i0  \tab\tab\tab loader, /V is always set by default.]\page\cf1 Glossary of terms used in this document [continued].\cf3\par
