\tab\tab\tab Hard-copy terminals can be configured for minimal \tab\tab\tab\tab support of buffer character delete acknowledgment or \tab\tab\tab\tab the method defined in the TOPS-10 operating system for \tab\tab\tab hard-copy terminals [which involves surrounding all \tab\tab\tab\tab deleted characters, printed in reverse order as they \tab\tab\tab\tab are deleted, by a leading and trailing \highlight3\\\highlight0  character].\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab The P?S/8 Logical Console Overlay supports several \tab\tab\tab\tab emulated terminals using hardware devices such as the \tab\tab\tab\tab VT8E and the PDP-12 VR-12/14 display oscilloscope.  \tab\tab\tab\tab Certain support parameters can be tailored for use with \tab\tab\tab these devices as required.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab Because P?S/8 supports an absolute standard for the \tab\tab\tab\tab console device, all conforming programs need not be \tab\tab\tab\tab involved in the details of the support.  Programs that \tab\tab\tab are non-conforming may have limited compatibility with \tab\tab\tab only certain console devices and/or machine models.\tab\tab\tab\tab [Note: Many older programs cannot be executed on \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab DECmate systems unless the internal console routines \tab\tab\tab\tab are upgraded to full P?S/8 compliance.  Example \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab programs are available in other documents to illustrate \tab\tab\tab conformance requirements.]\par
\par
System kernel\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab The system kernel on virtually all PDP-8 operating \tab\tab\tab\tab systems refers to memory locations 07600-0777 possibly \tab\tab\tab augmented by other memory areas depending on the \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab operating system.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab This hard system convention came about due to certain \tab\tab\tab\tab other software conventions that evolved over time.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab Paper-tape loading environment.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab The original paper-tape "operating system" addresses \tab\tab\tab\tab most of the issues.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab The original PDP-8 three-cycle data break devices \tab\tab\tab\tab reserve locations 07750-07755; programming the related \tab\tab\tab devices changes the contents of these locations out\tab  \tab\tab\tab of necessity:\page\cf1 Glossary of terms used in this document [continued].\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab 07750-07751 are used by either the DF32 or RF08 \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab fixed-head disk systems [which are mutually \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab exclusive].  Location 07750 is used to setup the \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab word count for a DMA transfer; location 07751 is \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab set to the desired transfer address [less one] as \tab\tab\tab\tab part of the basic programming of these devices.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab 07752-07753 serve the analogous functions as on \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab the DF32/RF08 for the TC58 .5 inch magnetic tape \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab controller.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab 07754-07755 serve the analogous functions for the \tab\tab\tab\tab TC01/TC08 DECtape controller.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab These important memory locations cannot be used by \tab\tab\tab\tab typical PDP-8 programs which do not address these \tab\tab\tab\tab specific devices.  As such, this area of memory is \tab\tab\tab\tab defined as off-limits to any general-purpose \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab program.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab The most fundamental usage of memory is the minimal \tab\tab\tab\tab loader function.  The earliest PDP-8 loading program is \tab\tab\tab the \b RIM\b0  loader designed to load into the rest of the \tab\tab\tab\tab page following the reserved three-cycle data break \tab\tab\tab\tab locations.  The \b RIM\b0  loader occupies locations 07756-\tab\tab\tab\tab 07776 and is started in 07756.  In terms of what is \tab\tab\tab\tab practical to be loaded into this small memory area, the \tab\tab\tab\b RIM\b0  loader represents the smallest possible memory \tab\tab\tab\tab usage.\par
\par
  \tab\tab\tab Note: Some years later, a user program was developed \tab\tab\tab\tab known as the "help" loader which is slightly smaller, \tab\tab\tab\tab but must be transiently loaded elsewhere in memory to \tab\tab\tab\tab be of any practical use.  It has a small user following \tab\tab\tab and is generally used to load the \b RIM\b0  loader and \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab perhaps the \b BIN \b0 loader using the shortest possible \tab\tab\tab\tab manual procedure to key in a paper-tape binary data \tab\tab\tab\tab loader.  The "help" loader is not practical for loading \tab\tab\tab in larger programs due to its inefficient use of paper-\tab\tab\tab tape compared to the \b RIM\b0  and \b BIN \b0 loaders.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab The \b RIM\b0  loader has many shortcomings which limits its \tab\tab\tab\tab usage to the most compromised situations such as when \tab\tab\tab\tab used with certain diagnostic programs designed to be \tab\tab\tab\tab capable of destroying all other locations in memory.  \tab\tab\tab\tab Generally, the \b RIM\b0  loader is used to load the \b BIN \tab\tab\tab\tab\b0 loader into memory for further program loading \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab purposes.\page\cf1 Glossary of terms used in this document [continued].\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab\tab The \b BIN \b0 loader has several advantages over the \b RIM\b0  \tab\tab\tab\tab loader:\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab 1)\tab The \b BIN \b0 loader supports automatic incremental \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab loading; The \b RIM\b0  loader requires each memory \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab location to be preceded by the loading address.  \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab The \b BIN \b0 loader only requires a loading address \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab when a non-adjacent memory area must be loaded.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab 2)\tab The \b BIN \b0 loader supports a checksum at the end of \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab the loading process.  If the accumulator is clear, \tab\tab\tab\tab the loading can be presumed to have been \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab successful; a non-zero accumulator indicates one \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab or more errors occurred during the loading \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab attempt.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab 3)\tab While both the \b RIM\b0  and \b BIN \b0 loaders support \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab loading into any memory field [the operator must \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab set the data field [DF] switches on the computer \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab front panel before starting the loading program]. \tab\tab\tab\tab only the \tab\b BIN \b0 loader supports FIELD settings \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab within the binary paper-tape contents.  Programs \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab can load into any memory field[s] as necessary.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab The exact memory locations used by the \b BIN \b0 loader\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab changed over time; multiple versions were released, \tab\tab\tab\tab each with slightly different memory usage.  It is \tab\tab\tab\tab believed that certain memory ranges within the \b BIN \tab\tab\tab\tab\b0 loader were reserved to support some short-lived \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab purpose of allowing locations in the last page of \tab\tab\tab\tab memory [07600-07777] to be available to certain \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab diagnostic programs.  With the release of additional \tab\tab\tab\tab\b BIN \b0 loader variants and the prevalence of storage-\tab\tab\tab\tab based operating systems, these conventions became \tab\tab\tab\tab obsolescent.  With few exceptions, diagnostic programs \tab\tab\tab do not reserve any memory locations in the range of \tab\tab\tab\tab 07600-07777 with the following exceptions:\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab 1)\tab The program may destroy all but 07756-07776 thus \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab preserving the \b RIM\b0  loader as discussed above.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab 2)\tab Certain programs may destroy certain locations in \tab\tab\tab\tab the range of 07600-07747 during program execution.  \tab\tab\tab\tab Depending on the specific \b BIN \b0 loader used, this \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab might be acceptable by design; the worst case is \par
\tab\tab\tab\tab the \b RIM\b0  loader must be used for all further \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab loading purposes.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab With little exception, all standard DEC paper-tape \tab\tab\tab\tab programs obey these conventions.\page\cf1 Glossary of terms used in this document [continued].\cf3\par
\cf0\par
  \tab\tab\tab Note: There are a few known "rogue" diagnostic programs \tab\tab\tab that do not conform to these conventions.  By not only \tab\tab\tab assuming a specific implementation of the \b BIN \b0 loader, \tab\tab\tab\tab but also loading initial data into the reserved memory \tab\tab\tab locations, these programs cannot be loaded by storage-\tab\tab\tab\tab based operating systems.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab These programs would not have been released had certain \tab\tab\tab DEC software managers been more competent.  All known \tab\tab\tab\tab examples are PDP-8/E-specific updates to existing \tab\tab\tab\tab diagnostics for earlier models.  The precursor programs \tab\tab\tab conform to the conventions [by merely storing into the \tab\tab\tab reserved \tab locations and not directly loading initial \tab\tab\tab\tab data there].\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab New staff members were not properly trained regarding \tab\tab\tab\tab the exact nature of the conventons; without proper \tab\tab\tab\tab management, not only were these mistakes not corrected, \tab\tab\tab they were released to DEC marketing.  Many customers \tab\tab\tab\tab had problems with them due to their inherent \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab incompatibility with prevailing DEC-supported storage-\tab\tab\tab\tab based operating systems [such as the Disk Monitor and \tab\tab\tab\tab OS/8].\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab P?S/8 supports a mechanism that can be used to remedy \tab\tab\tab\tab this situation in most cases; each program requires a \tab\tab\tab\tab slightly different \tab solution.  For more information, \tab\tab\tab\tab consult docmentation of the P?S/8 BIN utility in other \tab\tab\tab documents.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\b BIN \b0 loader startup convention.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab Location 07777 is reserved as the universal startup \tab\tab\tab\tab location for all versions of the \b BIN \b0 loader\tab .  It is \tab\tab\tab\tab implemented as a JMP instruction to the internal \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab starting location of the specific \b BIN \b0 loader \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab implementation used.\par
\par
\b\tab\tab\tab BIN \b0 loader implementations can encompass loading into \tab\tab\tab\tab locations 07600-07747 depending on the specific \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab version.  In truth, nearly then entire highest page of \tab\tab\tab memory [07600-07777] was eventually reserved in the \tab\tab\tab\tab sense that programs should avoid these locations.\par
\tab\tab\tab Storage system environments.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab While there are several viable single-user storage-\tab\tab\tab\tab based operating systems for the PDP-8, all such systems \tab\tab\tab reserve locations 07600-07777 with an exit to location \tab\tab\tab 07600 to restart the operating system.\page\cf1 Glossary of terms used in this document [continued].\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab\tab This includes the DECtape Library System, the Disk \tab\tab\tab\tab Monitor System, The R-L Monitor System, the Disk \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab Monitor System, DIBOL-8, POLY BASIC, P?S/8, OS/8 \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab [formerly PS/8], OS/78, OS/278, EDU-System 30, EDU-\tab\tab\tab\tab System 15/30,  and COS-300/310.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab Certain systems such as OS/8 and COS-300/310 require at \tab\tab\tab least 8K memory [12K in certain configurations].  The \tab\tab\tab\tab system kernel uses \i x\i0 7600-\i x\i0 7777 where \i x\i0  is 1 [or 1 and \tab\tab\tab\tab 2 if 12K memory minimum is required].  Due to sloppy \tab\tab\tab\tab system conventions, all memory fields are considered \tab\tab\tab\tab to have \i x\i0 7600-\i x\i0 7777 reserved; user and system programs \tab\tab\tab must avoid this area in all memory fields.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab P?S/8 requires 4K minimum for most configurations; for \tab\tab\tab certain configurations, a minimum of 8K is needed.  \tab\tab\tab\tab However, the extended memory segment reserved is \i x\i0 6000-\tab\tab\tab\i x\i0 7777 where \i x\i0  is the highest memory field available.  \tab\tab\tab\tab All existent extended memory in any lower memory fields \tab\tab\tab [assuming at least 12K is available] are available to \tab\tab\tab\tab system and user programs.  This is especially useful \tab\tab\tab\tab for applications that require contiguous 15-bit \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab addressing such as all programming for the FPP hardware \tab\tab\tab and various software-based runtime systems that emulate \tab\tab\tab a similar environment.  [Systems such as OS/8 \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab incorporate crude kludges to attempt to compensate for \tab\tab\tab bad design when programs such as Fortran IV are run; \tab\tab\tab\tab had OS/8 been designed for a memory model similar to\par
\tab\tab\tab P?S/8, these kludges would be unnecessary.]\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab Most system kernels, with the exception of the paper-\tab\tab\tab\tab tape software and the DECtape Library System, provide \tab\tab\tab\tab some form of system service to conforming progams.  \tab\tab\tab\tab [The DECtape Library System does have read/write \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab routines callable from outside of the system kernel, \tab\tab\tab\tab but the functionality of this system is extremely \tab\tab\tab\tab limited; saving images of memory and loading saved \tab\tab\tab\tab images of memory are the primary functions; all other \tab\tab\tab\tab operations are involved with either the creation of a \tab\tab\tab\tab starter system of maintenance of the system itself.]\page\cf1 Glossary of terms used in this document [continued].\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab\tab POLY BASIC, EDU-System 30 and EDU-System 15/30 are \tab\tab\tab\tab closed environments that only provide an environment to \tab\tab\tab edit, compile and run BASIC programs.\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab\tab The R-L Monitor System, as a raw implementation of an \tab\tab\tab\tab operating system, provides merely a framework for \tab\tab\tab\tab system programs.  When a system program is run, a list \tab\tab\tab of input files is passed in the system kernel along \tab\tab\tab\tab with a 12-bit value of the user-supplied =\i xxxx\i0  \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab expression.  The system does not provide any means to \tab\tab\tab\tab access the files, as the mechanism used to load the \tab\tab\tab\tab program image is merely a special-purpose loader for \tab\tab\tab\tab the image of the program; a system reboot routine is \tab\tab\tab\tab provided starting at 07600.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab Since the R-L Monitor only runs on TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 \tab\tab\tab DECtape [only drive 0 is supported], each program image \tab\tab\tab must contain its own internal DECtape read/write \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab subroutine [typically patterned after the system device \tab\tab\tab handler used internally by other parts of the operating \tab\tab\tab system only].\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab Conforming programs work with the R-L framework to \tab\tab\tab\tab process input files.  Where applicable, binary output \tab\tab\tab\tab is created to a dedicated output area defined for the \tab\tab\tab\tab purpose.  Despite these limitations, PDP-8 assembly \tab\tab\tab\tab programming and several high-level language \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab implementations are available.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab Note: The R-L Monitor System is the precursor to P?S/8.  \tab\tab\tab All of the limitations present were overcome; the \tab\tab\tab\tab system kernel provided read/write capability directly; \tab\tab\tab as such, P?S/8 is device-independent and runs on many \tab\tab\tab\tab configurations.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab The OS/8 "family" of operating systems are mostly \tab\tab\tab\tab compatible with each other to within internal \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab differences that are needed to run on specific models.  \tab\tab\tab There are confusing system names borne of a mix of \tab\tab\tab\tab marketing influences attempting to create a product \tab\tab\tab\tab without clear distinctions or an actual hardware \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab requirement where the specific version determines \tab\tab\tab\tab exactly which models the system will run on.\page\cf1 Glossary of terms used in this document [continued].\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab\tab Most notably, OS/78 Version 4 is designated for the \tab\tab\tab\tab DECmate I and OS/278 is designated for the DECmate II, \tab\tab\tab III and III+ [Version 2 is the only viable version; \tab\tab\tab\tab Version 1 was never a complete release and is only \tab\tab\tab\tab found on diagnostic systems that weren't updated].  \tab\tab\tab\tab However, neither of these systems completely function.  \tab\tab\tab The console hardware of the DECmates is sufficiently \tab\tab\tab\tab incompatible with former machines that many released \tab\tab\tab\tab programs have bugs and certain problems cannot be \tab\tab\tab\tab solved due to inadequacies of the kernel; despite \tab\tab\tab\tab requiring a kernel twice the size of the equivalent of \tab\tab\tab any of the above [or more] there is adequate memory \tab\tab\tab\tab space to fix these problems.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab Additional incompatibilities of internal system calls \tab\tab\tab\tab exist due to sloppy system maintenance.  A few \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab programs, such as Kermit-12, navigate around known \tab\tab\tab\tab differences by using safe values on parameters such as \tab\tab\tab maximum file size when opening a file.  The program \tab\tab\tab\tab performs its own check for CPU model to better adapt to \tab\tab\tab the differences of the DECmate environment [not \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab provided by the system kernel].\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab All versions of OS/8 have general problems with the \tab\tab\tab\tab directory structure and badly-designed system date \tab\tab\tab\tab handling.  As of this writing, OS/8 has no method for \tab\tab\tab\tab reckoning the current date for well over a decade.  \tab\tab\tab\tab [Arguments can be made this capability actually ran out \tab\tab\tab in 1978.]\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab The Disk Monitor System provides a convoluted series of \tab\tab\tab system components which are oriented around the few \tab\tab\tab\tab system programs available.  A read/write facility is \tab\tab\tab\tab available, but all operations are performed in an \tab\tab\tab\tab inefficient manner a single block at a time.  Every \tab\tab\tab\tab other system discussed here supports the concept of \tab\tab\tab\tab multiple block read/write transfers; the performance on \tab\tab\tab TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 DECtape [one of the few \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab configurations supported; the system cannot run on any \tab\tab\tab other devices other than DF32 and RF08 due to \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab incompatibility with newer hardware] is most notably \tab\tab\tab\tab poor on DECtape.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab The P?S/8 system kernel provides device-independent \tab\tab\tab\tab efficient multiple-block system device handler calls \tab\tab\tab\tab for any conforming program.  Various program loading \tab\tab\tab\tab modes are available ranging from a primitive rapid \tab\tab\tab\tab program load essentially an outgrowth of the R-L \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab Monitor loading method through a full implementation of \tab\tab\tab a P?S/8 system program.\page\cf1 Glossary of terms used in this document [continued].\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab\tab [Note: P?S/8 system programs can also define overlay \tab\tab\tab\tab structures.]\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab The P?S/8 system kernel also provides file information \tab\tab\tab that is an outgrowth of the R-L Monitor method of \tab\tab\tab\tab passing input files.  Output files are also supported \tab\tab\tab\tab through a provided counter of output versus input \tab\tab\tab\tab files.  [Up to 17 files can be passed with any number \tab\tab\tab\tab of input or output files.]\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab Program option switches are passed using the 36-bit \tab\tab\tab\tab model from TOPS-10 [implemented as three 12-bit words].  \tab\tab\tab Any and all options in the range of /A through /Z and \tab\tab\tab\tab /0 through /9 can be passed in any execution-class \tab\tab\tab\tab command line.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab The system date is available in memory [future releases \tab\tab\tab are changing the format for easier date reckoning; the \tab\tab\tab new date range is 01-Jan-1900 through 31-Dec-2411] to \tab\tab\tab\tab allow conforming programs to display the current date.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab P?S/8 supports an optional memory-based component known \tab\tab\tab as the Logical Console Overlay.  Conforming programs \tab\tab\tab\tab change the physical console [and system line printer] \tab\tab\tab\tab handling to logical calls to the Overlay if present.  A \tab\tab\tab control word in the system kernel provides all \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab information on interfacing with this component [when in \tab\tab\tab effect].\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab Various additional control bits are implemented in the \tab\tab\tab P?S/8 system kernel.  A notable example is the presence \tab\tab\tab of a logical abort bit.  This feature allows proper \tab\tab\tab\tab operation on DECmates that lack compatible console \tab\tab\tab\tab hardware [which cannot pass back the information \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab regarding program abort when exiting to the system \tab\tab\tab\tab kernel at 07600].\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab P?S/8 is under major development as of this writing.  A \tab\tab\tab new optional SHELL overlay will be implemented to allow \tab\tab\tab an advanced directory structure and radically different \tab\tab\tab command processor mechanism reminiscent of other \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab operating systems.  A control bit is available to allow \tab\tab\tab conforming programs the ability to interface with the \tab\tab\tab\tab greatly expanded system communications and information \tab\tab\tab structure of the SHELL environment.  Additional system \tab\tab\tab services work with information that is available either \tab\tab\tab virtually or in memory depending on the specific \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab configuration.  Conforming programs have access to a \tab\tab\tab\tab variety of command-specific parameters; the scope of \tab\tab\tab\tab the available information far exceeds that of OS/8, yet \tab\tab\tab the system can still be minimally functional on a 4K \tab\tab\tab\tab machine.\page\cf1 Glossary of terms used in this document [continued].\cf3\par
\cf0\par
\tab\tab\tab Standalone programs need not be concerned with the \tab\tab\tab\tab particulars of either the basic P?S/8 or SHELL \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab environments; the only proviso is the program cannot be \tab\tab\tab expected to run on DECmates.  Fully conforming programs \tab\tab\tab can run on any configuration of P?S/8 including all \tab\tab\tab\tab DECmates.\par
\par
System Line Printer.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab The system line printer is an optional component of any \tab\tab\tab P?S/8 system, generally consisting of a high-speed \tab\tab\tab\tab hard-copy printing unit connected by a separate\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab interface.  The standard line printer is device 66 with \tab\tab\tab a parallel interface that conforms to Centronics \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab parallel and related standards as made by a variety of \tab\tab\tab companies such as Data Products [both with and without \tab\tab\tab DEC branding that changes little to no internal \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab components].  [Note: DEC sold a Centronics printer with \tab\tab\tab the DEC branding on the outside and a cooling fan\par
\tab\tab\tab removed from the inside; no other changes were made to \tab\tab\tab this device.  Informed users saved money purchasing the \tab\tab\tab Centronics variant.]\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab P?S/8 universally supports the device 66 line printer \tab\tab\tab\tab as required.  Other printers can be supported through \tab\tab\tab\tab customization of the Logical Console Overlay, which \tab\tab\tab\tab allows flexible support of several categories of device \tab\tab\tab including serial printers that require reverse-channel \tab\tab\tab flow-control support by the use of the\par
\tab\tab\tab\highlight3 Control-S\highlight0 /\highlight3 Control-Q\highlight0  industry-standard protocol.  The \tab\tab\tab\tab device 66 printer support is generally enhanced by the \tab\tab\tab use of software buffering which can produce dramatic\tab\tab\tab\tab improvement of printing throughput especially on higher \tab\tab\tab speed models.  [By way of comparison, OS/8 cannot drive \tab\tab\tab a device 66 line printer beyond 300 lines per minute \tab\tab\tab\tab while P?S/8, with the Logical Console Overlay enabled, \tab\tab\tab has successfully printed on suitable printers at over \tab\tab\tab\tab 1200 lines per minute.]\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab Note: Many older programs cannot support printers other \tab\tab\tab than the device 66 standard models.  By conforming with \tab\tab\tab P?S/8 standards, any program can be made to work with\tab\tab\tab\tab all supported printers.  [As in the case of the P?S/8\par
\tab\tab\tab system console, example programs are available in \tab\tab\tab\tab separate documents to illustrate conformance \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab requirements.]\page\cf1 Glossary of terms used in this document [continued].\cf3\par
\cf0\par
TFS\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab The TFS [or Tiny File System] is the name of the \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab primary user file system used in the R-L Monitor, \tab\tab\tab\tab DIBOL-8 and P?S/8.  TFS files are fixed-length and \tab\tab\tab\tab always stored in 16 contiguous blocks.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab Since the file structure is deliberately simplified, \tab\tab\tab\tab the entire TFS directory structure can be completely \tab\tab\tab\tab cached in the keyboard monitor and related utilities.  \tab\tab\tab This allows high-speed access to all TFS files \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab regardless of physical storage position.  The TFS \tab\tab\tab\tab directory is only updated when necessary; if a file \tab\tab\tab\tab slot is reused, there is no need to update the \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab directory.  If a file physically adjacent to a \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab previously-loaded file requires loading into the \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab editing buffer, there is no need to perform a physical \tab\tab\tab directory access when changing files.  This method of \tab\tab\tab\tab file access offers profound performance improvement \tab\tab\tab\tab on serial access devices such as DECtape and LINCtape \tab\tab\tab\tab over more conventional approaches.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab Note: The R-L Monitor System and DIBOL-8 do not support \tab\tab\tab logical device units; the only supported storage device \tab\tab\tab is a single DECtape drive connected to a TC01/TC08 \tab\tab\tab\tab DECtape controller.  The starting block of the TFS \tab\tab\tab\tab directory on these systems can be any storage block \tab\tab\tab\tab without restriction.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab P?S/8 places a boundary restriction on the starting \tab\tab\tab\tab block number of the TFS directory; all files must fall \tab\tab\tab on boundaries that are even multiples of eight blocks.  \tab\tab\tab This allows P?S/8 to supply file starting block and \tab\tab\tab\tab logical unit number [in the range of 0-7] in a single \tab\tab\tab\tab 12-bit word to any conforming system program.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab TFS \i slurp \i0 binary format files from the R-L Monitor \tab\tab\tab\tab System and DIBOL-8 are fully compatible with P?S/8; \tab\tab\tab\tab however, text files must be converted using the R-L \tab\tab\tab\tab "FIXUP" program available from the developers of P?S/8.  \tab\tab\tab Once this is accomplished, all of the files can be \tab\tab\tab\tab transferred to P?S/8-based DECtapes to allow all P?S/8 \tab\tab\tab system programs to use these files.  The memory-\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab resident commands of the keyboard monitor can directly \tab\tab\tab access the files on DECtape after the FIXUP process has \tab\tab\tab been completed.  This allows a complete file migration \tab\tab\tab to P?S/8 from these older systems; two DECtape drives \tab\tab\tab\tab are required for the file transfer.\par
